WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:09.010
- Hi, everyone, and thank you
for coming to this discussion

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:12.080
of grant and fellowship applications.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:14.740
I'm Steve Pitti, professor
of history, American studies,

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:15.900
and ethnicity, race and migration,

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:19.080
and I direct Yale's Center
for the Study of Race,

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:21.420
Indigeneity and Transnational Migration.

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:24.640
I want to thank our staff,
Maggie Katz and Nicole Edwards,

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:27.930
for their help putting
this event together today.

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:29.390
This session is gonna focus on

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:32.530
identifying external and
internal opportunities

00:00:32.530 --> 00:00:34.920
of interest to the RITM community,

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:36.820
on identifying application strategies

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:39.740
and the value of pursuing
those opportunities

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:41.830
for graduate and professional students.

00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:44.230
I'm really grateful for the presence

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:47.010
of Dean Robert Harper-Mangels,

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:47.910
who has been a dean in

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:52.010
the Yale Graduate School of
Arts and Sciences since 2006.

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:53.490
He is currently associate dean

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:56.490
for admissions and
financial support at Yale.

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:59.060
In this position, he has
primary responsibility

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:00.990
for overseeing student fellowships

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:03.530
and external funding applications.

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:05.900
Dean Harper-Mangels has a
bachelor's degree in music

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:09.000
and psychology from Wesleyan University

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.980
and also a PhD in
neuroscience and behavior

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:15.030
from the University of
Massachusetts at Amherst.

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:17.210
Thank you so much, Dean Harper-Mangels,

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:18.900
for being here with us today,

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:21.060
really appreciate your willingness to come

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:24.513
and talk to us about applying for grants,

00:01:25.370 --> 00:01:27.810
which I think is gonna be
of great use to all of us.

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:29.630
Thank you very much for being here today.

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:32.070
For those of you who are present,

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:35.040
please feel free to put
questions in the Q and A

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:36.750
and we'll try to get those

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:38.550
to Dean Harper-Mangels at the end.

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:40.100
Thanks.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:41.930
- Thanks, Steve. Happy to be here today.

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:44.980
We're gonna talk about applying for grants

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:49.090
and the first thing that I
would like to address is,

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:51.210
actually, the first thing I'd
like to address is why my,

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:54.060
here we go, getting my
slides to move forward,

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:55.800
is why you should apply for grants.

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:59.830
A lot of students think that
they have a full fellowship,

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:02.220
they have six years of
funding while they're at Yale,

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:03.780
there's a lot of opportunities

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:07.390
to get a Yale funding
for travel and research,

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:12.010
so why should they spend the
time applying for these grants?

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:13.440
And the first thing I would say is

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.730
that this is a really
important career skill

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:18.600
for all of you to be
developing at this point.

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:20.310
When you think about applying for a grant,

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:23.240
what you're really doing
is promoting your work.

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:25.970
You're trying to get people excited

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:29.100
about the kinds of things
that you want to do

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:31.370
to the degree that they
would like to support it

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:32.640
in some way,

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:35.470
and this goes beyond just
getting grant funding

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:37.200
for the work that you're doing now.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.170
If you think about applying for a job,

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:41.660
if you think about the kinds of careers

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:43.890
that you might have in the future,

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:45.700
you're often trying to sell people

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:47.650
on the work that you're doing
and getting them excited about

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:50.173
how they can support what you want to do.

00:02:50.173 --> 00:02:52.780
This is a great skill to be developing

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:53.830
at this point in time,

00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:56.530
when you have this safety
net, when you have a stipend,

00:02:56.530 --> 00:02:58.350
when you have other forms
of support available

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:00.810
so that if you go out and you
don't get the fellowships,

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:01.643
it's okay.

00:03:01.643 --> 00:03:02.730
We can still support you,

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:05.390
you can still do the
work that you need to do,

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:06.480
but this is a great time

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:08.973
to just start working on these skills.

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:12.770
One of the things you
wanna remember is that,

00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:14.793
I like to say funding begets funding.

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:18.930
First of all, if you're
applying for a grant

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:20.380
and somebody is looking at your CV

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:22.457
and they're looking at your
background and seeing that,

00:03:22.457 --> 00:03:23.680
"Oh, this person's been supported

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:25.090
by this agency to do this work.

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:27.510
They've been supported in the
past by this other agency.

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:29.500
They look like they're
good stewards of the money.

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:31.990
They look like when they say
they're gonna do something,

00:03:31.990 --> 00:03:33.200
they get it done.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:35.430
We feel better about
supporting this student too

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:37.020
because we think that they will do

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:39.210
what they say they're going to do,"

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:40.830
and it also gives you an opportunity,

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:44.070
a valuable opportunity to
establish a relationship

00:03:44.070 --> 00:03:47.790
with funding agencies, so that,

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:50.360
and I use this example a lot
with my science students,

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:53.300
if you've applied to the NIH for a grant

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:54.970
as a pre-doctoral grant

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.290
and you are funded and you do the work

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:00.000
and you complete your PhD,

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.380
and then you go and you get a post-doc

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:04.440
and you apply for a post-doctoral grant

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:06.170
and they agree to support that,

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:07.980
then when you're a junior faculty member

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:10.960
and you're applying for
that first faculty grant,

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:12.147
they're gonna look and they're gonna say,

00:04:12.147 --> 00:04:14.680
"We supported this person
when they were a pre-doc

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:17.720
and we supported this person
when they were a post-doc.

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:19.210
They've done what they
said they were going to do.

00:04:19.210 --> 00:04:20.790
They've been successful throughout.

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:22.220
Let's continue to back this horse.

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:25.370
Let's continue to provide
support to this person.

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:27.131
They've been a good bet so far,

00:04:27.131 --> 00:04:30.060
so we should keep on with them."

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:32.250
Establishing a relationship
with a funding agency

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:34.810
that might be useful to
you in your future work

00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:36.020
is a good thing to do, and again,

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:38.653
now is the time to establish that,

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:42.970
and then there were just some
practical benefits to you

00:04:42.970 --> 00:04:45.260
as a doctoral student.

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:48.580
The first, of course, is that
if you win an outside award,

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:50.370
whatever it is they're providing you with,

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:52.380
they'll provide you with a top-up

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:53.730
and bring you to the full stipend,

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:57.300
plus you'll get $4,000 above
the current stipend levels.

00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:01.560
You'll make a little bit
more money, or alternatively,

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:02.880
if it's a travel award

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:05.163
and you want to go abroad
in a teaching year,

00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:08.190
you obviously aren't going to be paid

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:11.163
if you're not teaching in,
say, your third or fourth year,

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:14.030
but that's why funding
gives you the flexibility

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:16.490
to buy out your teaching
or postpone your teaching

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:18.900
and support yourself while
you do field work in year four

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:20.100
so that you don't need to go

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:22.680
and use your dissertation fellowship,

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:24.230
which you'd really like to preserve

00:05:24.230 --> 00:05:26.210
and have available to
you when you come back

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:29.193
so you do have that teaching-free year

00:05:29.193 --> 00:05:30.693
that you can start writing in.

00:05:33.070 --> 00:05:36.120
I just want to talk about a few things.

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:37.140
How do you get started?

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:38.100
If I've convinced you

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:40.010
that you should be applying for awards,

00:05:40.010 --> 00:05:41.630
how do you start this process,

00:05:41.630 --> 00:05:44.070
but what should you be looking to do?

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:48.660
Step one obviously is just
finding fellowships to apply for.

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:53.480
There's a database here at
Yale, at funding.yale.edu,

00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:56.810
which mostly contains competitive awards

00:05:56.810 --> 00:05:58.950
that are funded through Yale.

00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:01.640
These are usually endowed
funds that are given to Yale

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:04.680
to support travel or
research in certain areas

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:07.780
that you would apply for
on a competitive basis,

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:08.970
but it's Yale's money.

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:11.390
It also would include fellowships

00:06:11.390 --> 00:06:15.470
that for which some sort of a
Yale endorsement is required,

00:06:15.470 --> 00:06:17.240
so that if there's, say,

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:19.570
a limited number of students
from Yale are permitted

00:06:19.570 --> 00:06:21.650
to apply for some external fellowship

00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:25.310
and we have to engage in some
sort of a nominating process,

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:26.880
you'll find most of those

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:29.403
on the Yale grants and funding page.

00:06:30.710 --> 00:06:32.940
Grantforward.com is probably

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:34.640
the single best site that I've ever seen.

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:36.130
It's certainly not the
only site out there,

00:06:36.130 --> 00:06:38.470
but it's a very comprehensive site.

00:06:38.470 --> 00:06:40.570
It contains large awards, small awards,

00:06:40.570 --> 00:06:42.593
federal awards, private awards.

00:06:43.830 --> 00:06:45.260
I think I went in the other day

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:48.210
and typed in race and migration

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:50.823
and it came up with 3,000 results.

00:06:52.128 --> 00:06:53.830
You will have to play
with it a little bit,

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:55.810
play with different keywords to focus in

00:06:55.810 --> 00:06:58.060
on the things that are
most important to you,

00:06:58.950 --> 00:07:03.000
but this is a site that Yale
is a sponsor of the site.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:05.700
You should, from any Yale.edu address,

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:08.153
be able to log in and make use of it,

00:07:09.070 --> 00:07:10.400
and you'll see everything in there,

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:14.450
from your large federal
$35,000 full-year awards

00:07:14.450 --> 00:07:18.110
to $500 to support research

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:23.480
for people who are studying
the use of corn in society

00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:26.943
and whose grandparents or
parents were born in Greece.

00:07:28.040 --> 00:07:31.370
There are some very small,
very specific grants out there

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:34.460
and large federal awards out there.

00:07:34.460 --> 00:07:37.030
GrantForward seems to have them all.

00:07:37.030 --> 00:07:41.020
The grants.gov website is a
sort of a central clearing house

00:07:41.020 --> 00:07:44.120
for most of the
federally-funded fellowships

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:46.240
that you might apply for,

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:48.700
and I would just urge you,

00:07:48.700 --> 00:07:53.340
if you're looking into
travel awards to go abroad,

00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:57.370
you should look also at
the country websites,

00:07:57.370 --> 00:08:01.040
look to see if the country
itself has fellowships

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:03.740
to attract students to
come to the country.

00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:04.840
Not everybody does,

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:08.850
but Germany and Japan have big agencies

00:08:08.850 --> 00:08:11.520
that provide a lot of money for this.

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:13.160
There's the InterAmerican Foundation

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:15.993
that will fund travel to
Central and South America.

00:08:17.961 --> 00:08:20.533
There's a lot of ways to find grants

00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:23.820
that might be of interest to you,

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:26.420
and finally, make use of the
people in your department.

00:08:26.420 --> 00:08:28.680
Ask your faculty. Ask
the most senior students.

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:31.620
Find out what the other
people have applied for

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:34.180
or what agencies they've been
involved with in the past

00:08:34.180 --> 00:08:36.883
and see what they have to say.

00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:41.290
If the most important message I have today

00:08:41.290 --> 00:08:44.720
is that it's important for
you to apply for fellowships

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:45.960
at this stage of your career,

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:47.290
that it's great training for you,

00:08:47.290 --> 00:08:49.340
that it's really good practice,

00:08:49.340 --> 00:08:51.340
the second most important message I have

00:08:51.340 --> 00:08:53.900
is that when you're
applying for a fellowship,

00:08:53.900 --> 00:08:57.630
it's really critical
to understand the needs

00:08:57.630 --> 00:09:00.870
and the motivation of the
agency that you're applying to.

00:09:00.870 --> 00:09:03.910
There are lots of organizations
out there that have money

00:09:03.910 --> 00:09:07.020
that they're willing to give
to you to promote your work,

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:09.630
but they all have a different
reason for doing it,

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:11.077
and it's all well and good to say,

00:09:11.077 --> 00:09:12.280
"Well, I want to do this

00:09:12.280 --> 00:09:14.030
and I'm interested in
that and if you fund me,

00:09:14.030 --> 00:09:15.910
here are the things that
I'll be able to do,"

00:09:15.910 --> 00:09:19.123
but does it speak to the
mission of the agency?

00:09:20.050 --> 00:09:22.620
I'll just give you two quick examples.

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:25.643
I have students in the
sciences who will apply,

00:09:25.643 --> 00:09:27.340
they work on a project and they'll apply

00:09:27.340 --> 00:09:28.983
to both the NIH and the NSF.

00:09:31.430 --> 00:09:34.500
The mission of the NIH is to cure disease

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:36.600
and relieve human suffering.

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:39.010
The NSF has that as a mission as well,

00:09:39.010 --> 00:09:42.940
but the NSF also has more
of an educational outreach,

00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:46.840
a science literacy
component to their mission,

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:49.190
so then you can't just
take the same project

00:09:49.190 --> 00:09:50.883
and submit it to both agencies.

00:09:51.930 --> 00:09:55.160
With the NIH it's really what
is it that I'm going to do

00:09:55.160 --> 00:09:58.490
and how is this going to
lead to curing disease.

00:09:58.490 --> 00:10:00.920
With the NSF it's what am I going to do

00:10:00.920 --> 00:10:04.300
and how is it going to
cure disease, but also,

00:10:04.300 --> 00:10:06.770
how is the training that
I'm going to receive

00:10:06.770 --> 00:10:10.060
going to allow me to go out
and improve science literacy?

00:10:10.060 --> 00:10:12.290
How am I going to share
the results of my work

00:10:12.290 --> 00:10:14.570
with an audience beyond
the people in my lab

00:10:14.570 --> 00:10:18.060
and the people in the scientific
community that I work with?

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:22.290
Another example is the Fulbright
and the Fulbright-Hays.

00:10:22.290 --> 00:10:25.910
Fulbright is funded by
the Department of State

00:10:25.910 --> 00:10:28.160
and it's a cultural exchange grant.

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:31.600
The Fulbright-Hays is funded
by the Department of Education

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:32.750
and it's really more of

00:10:33.868 --> 00:10:36.370
a language and cultural literacy grant.

00:10:36.370 --> 00:10:38.100
Again, you can have the same project

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:40.800
and apply to both agencies for funding,

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:43.210
but in the case of Fulbright
you really need to explain

00:10:43.210 --> 00:10:46.110
how in the course of doing this project

00:10:46.110 --> 00:10:47.710
it will promote cultural exchange.

00:10:47.710 --> 00:10:48.910
These are where the opportunities

00:10:48.910 --> 00:10:50.670
for cultural exchange will be.

00:10:50.670 --> 00:10:53.020
Fulbright-Hays doesn't care
about cultural exchange

00:10:53.020 --> 00:10:55.320
so much as they care
about the degree to which

00:10:55.320 --> 00:10:57.390
you are becoming a knowledge expert

00:10:57.390 --> 00:11:00.170
in the language and the
culture of that country.

00:11:00.170 --> 00:11:02.500
They don't necessarily care
what you're giving back.

00:11:02.500 --> 00:11:04.100
They want to know what you're getting

00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:06.370
so that you can come
back to the United States

00:11:06.370 --> 00:11:10.320
and be a resource to the US government

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:11.760
if they need to talk to somebody

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:13.693
about conditions in that country.

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:17.010
Same project but a different spin on it

00:11:17.010 --> 00:11:19.210
so that you can speak to
the mission of the agency

00:11:19.210 --> 00:11:21.613
and convince them that
they want to support you.

00:11:23.310 --> 00:11:24.610
In understanding their mission,

00:11:24.610 --> 00:11:27.060
you also want to think
about who at the agency

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:29.060
is going to be reading your application.

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:35.230
Are these PhD-level scientists
who work in your field?

00:11:35.230 --> 00:11:37.820
If so, less explanation about

00:11:37.820 --> 00:11:39.240
why you're doing what you're doing

00:11:39.240 --> 00:11:42.500
or specifically what you're
doing may be appropriate.

00:11:42.500 --> 00:11:44.930
You'll want to get into more
detail about the methodology

00:11:44.930 --> 00:11:47.250
and maybe some of the
nuances about the work

00:11:47.250 --> 00:11:48.550
that you'll be engaged in.

00:11:49.910 --> 00:11:54.910
These may be PhD-level people
who are not in your field.

00:11:55.870 --> 00:11:57.800
If you apply to Fulbright,

00:11:57.800 --> 00:11:59.800
the Fulbright applications, for example,

00:11:59.800 --> 00:12:02.580
are all reviewed by country.

00:12:02.580 --> 00:12:03.690
The first round of review,

00:12:03.690 --> 00:12:08.640
if you've applied to France
and you're interested in poetry

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:10.910
at the time of the Renaissance,

00:12:10.910 --> 00:12:13.600
French poetry at the
time of the Renaissance,

00:12:13.600 --> 00:12:15.597
the first people who are
going to read your application

00:12:15.597 --> 00:12:19.620
are gonna be three PhD-level faculty

00:12:19.620 --> 00:12:21.603
who studied France,

00:12:22.520 --> 00:12:26.000
but one of them might
focus on medieval history,

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:29.500
one of them might be
interested in modern history,

00:12:29.500 --> 00:12:33.283
and maybe one of them is
doing religious studies.

00:12:34.690 --> 00:12:38.830
Educated people, yes, but not
necessarily in your field.

00:12:38.830 --> 00:12:40.190
In that case,

00:12:40.190 --> 00:12:42.340
you can't assume that
they'll understand everything

00:12:42.340 --> 00:12:43.500
you're talking about right up front.

00:12:43.500 --> 00:12:45.360
You need to provide a little more detail

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:47.900
about what it is you want to do,

00:12:47.900 --> 00:12:50.150
why this is interesting,
why it's important,

00:12:50.150 --> 00:12:52.990
what kind of gaps you're
filling in the knowledge

00:12:52.990 --> 00:12:54.610
in that field.

00:12:54.610 --> 00:12:58.720
I have a number of students
now who are applying to P.E.O.,

00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:02.110
which is a woman's
organization which promotes,

00:13:02.110 --> 00:13:04.793
provides funding for
women in higher education.

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:07.150
It's quite likely that the people

00:13:07.150 --> 00:13:08.870
who will be reading those applications

00:13:08.870 --> 00:13:11.993
may be doctors, lawyers, CEOs,

00:13:12.940 --> 00:13:16.390
highly-educated people,
but not necessarily PhDs,

00:13:16.390 --> 00:13:18.840
and certainly not people who
are working in the fields

00:13:18.840 --> 00:13:21.040
that the students are applying in.

00:13:21.040 --> 00:13:22.920
Just think about who your audience is

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:25.530
and what level of detail
you need to provide

00:13:25.530 --> 00:13:27.410
in order to get them up to speed

00:13:27.410 --> 00:13:31.450
on what it is you're studying
and why this is interesting.

00:13:31.450 --> 00:13:32.760
You can't assume that just because

00:13:32.760 --> 00:13:33.960
you think it's interesting,

00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:35.390
that they're going to
think it's interesting

00:13:35.390 --> 00:13:39.520
without you pointing them
to the different cues

00:13:39.520 --> 00:13:41.490
that they would say, "Oh yeah, no.

00:13:41.490 --> 00:13:44.190
Now that you've explained
it to me, this makes sense.

00:13:44.190 --> 00:13:45.640
This does sound interesting."

00:13:47.980 --> 00:13:50.470
Once you've identified
something you want to apply for

00:13:50.470 --> 00:13:55.210
and you've thought about
the motivation of the agency

00:13:55.210 --> 00:13:56.600
and providing the funding,

00:13:56.600 --> 00:13:58.520
there's some other things you can do.

00:13:58.520 --> 00:14:00.570
First of all, has anybody
else in your department

00:14:00.570 --> 00:14:02.570
applied for this award in the past?

00:14:02.570 --> 00:14:04.910
It may be that your registrar has access

00:14:04.910 --> 00:14:06.890
to old applications.

00:14:06.890 --> 00:14:08.447
It may be that some of the students

00:14:08.447 --> 00:14:09.280
who have applied are still here

00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:11.500
and you can talk to them
about their experience

00:14:11.500 --> 00:14:13.470
and whether or not they
received any feedback

00:14:13.470 --> 00:14:15.790
and have any insights into what works

00:14:15.790 --> 00:14:18.080
or what doesn't work with an application.

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:19.950
Maybe some of your faculty have served

00:14:19.950 --> 00:14:22.050
as reviewers for this organization

00:14:22.050 --> 00:14:23.880
and they certainly then
would be a great resource

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:26.663
to tell you what the
reviewers are looking for.

00:14:27.670 --> 00:14:30.230
If you're applying for
international travel,

00:14:30.230 --> 00:14:31.940
the MacMillan Center is a great resource.

00:14:31.940 --> 00:14:33.210
They've got knowledge experts there

00:14:33.210 --> 00:14:34.470
who can talk to you about

00:14:34.470 --> 00:14:36.280
what's going on in individual countries

00:14:36.280 --> 00:14:38.530
and things that you
might need to think about

00:14:39.840 --> 00:14:41.940
and problems that you
might need to address

00:14:41.940 --> 00:14:43.540
in the application, pitfalls or problems

00:14:43.540 --> 00:14:45.760
that you might need to overcome

00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:48.983
that they can say that an
agency might want to see

00:14:48.983 --> 00:14:50.583
that you're knowledgeable about.

00:14:51.930 --> 00:14:54.660
The Graduate Writing Lab,

00:14:54.660 --> 00:14:56.820
I want to say it's an
outstanding resource.

00:14:56.820 --> 00:14:59.630
I have not sent anybody
there who's ever come back

00:14:59.630 --> 00:15:01.860
and told me that it was
a waste of their time.

00:15:01.860 --> 00:15:03.643
I've had a lot of people come back and say

00:15:03.643 --> 00:15:06.320
that they were really very helpful.

00:15:06.320 --> 00:15:08.270
They do a number of different things,

00:15:08.270 --> 00:15:10.570
including peer review, writing groups,

00:15:10.570 --> 00:15:12.513
one-on-one mentoring with tutors.

00:15:14.100 --> 00:15:17.330
They're a fantastic resource
if you're applying for an award

00:15:17.330 --> 00:15:18.517
where the people who are reviewing it

00:15:18.517 --> 00:15:22.840
are not necessarily going
to be squarely in your field

00:15:22.840 --> 00:15:25.420
because now you're talking
to an educated audience

00:15:25.420 --> 00:15:27.570
that will say, "Yeah,
your writing is good,

00:15:27.570 --> 00:15:29.010
this looks like a tight proposal,

00:15:29.010 --> 00:15:31.390
but I'm not sure I really understand

00:15:31.390 --> 00:15:32.300
what it is you're doing.

00:15:32.300 --> 00:15:34.980
I think maybe you need to provide

00:15:34.980 --> 00:15:36.860
a little more information just up front,

00:15:36.860 --> 00:15:41.860
couple of sentences to help
orient the non-expert reader."

00:15:43.130 --> 00:15:45.860
The Graduate Writing Lab can
be a great source for that

00:15:45.860 --> 00:15:48.520
in a way that your advisor, for example,

00:15:48.520 --> 00:15:51.350
might not be able to help you
because they do what you do

00:15:51.350 --> 00:15:55.330
and they're going to
understand what you're doing.

00:15:55.330 --> 00:15:57.810
They'll be filling in
any gaps that existed

00:15:57.810 --> 00:15:59.630
in the writing in their own heads.

00:15:59.630 --> 00:16:02.520
The Graduate Writing Lab
won't be able to do that.

00:16:02.520 --> 00:16:05.690
They can ask you questions
and have you go back

00:16:05.690 --> 00:16:08.223
and rewrite it and fill
in those gaps yourself.

00:16:10.260 --> 00:16:11.210
In some cases,

00:16:11.210 --> 00:16:13.810
the funding agencies may encourage you

00:16:13.810 --> 00:16:16.370
to reach out to them
directly before applying,

00:16:16.370 --> 00:16:18.830
and again, remember that they have money,

00:16:18.830 --> 00:16:20.880
they're supporting some cause,

00:16:20.880 --> 00:16:22.803
they want successful applicants,

00:16:23.740 --> 00:16:24.980
so it's not always the case

00:16:24.980 --> 00:16:26.570
they don't all have the
bandwidth to do this,

00:16:26.570 --> 00:16:28.620
but many of them will actually
want you to reach out to them

00:16:28.620 --> 00:16:31.300
and say, "Hey, this is
what I'm working on.

00:16:31.300 --> 00:16:32.270
This is something I think would be

00:16:32.270 --> 00:16:33.320
of interest to your agency.

00:16:33.320 --> 00:16:36.310
Is this something that
would fly as a proposal?"

00:16:36.310 --> 00:16:38.087
And they can give you feedback.

00:16:38.087 --> 00:16:40.050
"Yeah, this sounds really interesting.

00:16:40.050 --> 00:16:42.610
We'd be more likely to
fund this than that.

00:16:42.610 --> 00:16:44.930
You might want to steer
it in this direction,"

00:16:44.930 --> 00:16:48.200
but if they invite you to reach out,

00:16:48.200 --> 00:16:50.160
I encourage you to do so.

00:16:50.160 --> 00:16:53.800
You can get a lot of really
valuable bits of information

00:16:53.800 --> 00:16:55.880
directly from them about
what they would consider

00:16:55.880 --> 00:16:57.483
to be a successful application,

00:16:58.470 --> 00:16:59.550
and I just want to add,

00:16:59.550 --> 00:17:01.840
I'll have my contact information
at the end of these slides,

00:17:01.840 --> 00:17:03.100
but you can certainly feel free

00:17:03.100 --> 00:17:05.170
to reach out to me at any time.

00:17:05.170 --> 00:17:07.570
I may not know about the specific award

00:17:07.570 --> 00:17:08.560
that you're applying for,

00:17:08.560 --> 00:17:11.600
but I do have quite a bit of experience

00:17:11.600 --> 00:17:14.060
with a variety of fellowship applications

00:17:14.060 --> 00:17:15.717
and I can certainly help you figure out

00:17:15.717 --> 00:17:18.613
how to get the answers if
you can't get them yourself.

00:17:22.230 --> 00:17:25.170
I'll talk a little bit
about some of the elements

00:17:25.170 --> 00:17:27.050
that would go into an application,

00:17:27.050 --> 00:17:28.290
and again, they're all different.

00:17:28.290 --> 00:17:30.853
This is not going to apply
to every application,

00:17:31.820 --> 00:17:33.830
but almost certainly there's gonna be

00:17:33.830 --> 00:17:35.590
some sort of a research proposal,

00:17:35.590 --> 00:17:38.320
just what it is that you want to do,

00:17:38.320 --> 00:17:41.743
and very often a personal
statement and reference letters,

00:17:42.908 --> 00:17:45.870
and I'll talk about
those each a little bit,

00:17:45.870 --> 00:17:47.130
and then depending on the award,

00:17:47.130 --> 00:17:49.800
they may want transcripts,
they may want a CV.

00:17:49.800 --> 00:17:52.040
Some awards will ask
you to provide a budget

00:17:52.040 --> 00:17:56.240
and sometimes there are
short answer essays,

00:17:56.240 --> 00:17:59.260
essay prompts that they
might ask you to complete.

00:17:59.260 --> 00:18:00.700
I'll just talk about each of these

00:18:00.700 --> 00:18:02.103
in a little bit more detail.

00:18:03.403 --> 00:18:07.130
The research proposal,
it's what you would expect.

00:18:07.130 --> 00:18:09.410
What do you want to do and
why do you want to do it

00:18:09.410 --> 00:18:11.520
and how do you plan to do it,

00:18:11.520 --> 00:18:13.560
and it's surprising to me sometimes

00:18:13.560 --> 00:18:16.590
how many people will start these essays

00:18:16.590 --> 00:18:19.310
and not cover those three elements.

00:18:19.310 --> 00:18:21.580
They sort of launch into
how they're going to do it

00:18:21.580 --> 00:18:24.400
before they've really
explained what they want to do

00:18:24.400 --> 00:18:26.700
and they never address
why they want to do it.

00:18:28.260 --> 00:18:31.580
You really start big, and again,

00:18:31.580 --> 00:18:33.160
the amount of space you spend on this

00:18:33.160 --> 00:18:36.100
depends in part on the
application requirements.

00:18:36.100 --> 00:18:37.920
Is this a two-page proposal?

00:18:37.920 --> 00:18:39.420
Is it a 10-page proposal?

00:18:39.420 --> 00:18:41.410
That gives you clues
to the level of detail

00:18:41.410 --> 00:18:45.440
that they might be looking
for, and then, again,

00:18:45.440 --> 00:18:47.120
thinking about the audience
that's reading this

00:18:47.120 --> 00:18:49.560
and how expert they are in your field,

00:18:49.560 --> 00:18:51.420
you want to provide some description of

00:18:51.420 --> 00:18:52.790
this is what I'm interested in,

00:18:52.790 --> 00:18:55.420
this is why I'm interested in doing this,

00:18:55.420 --> 00:18:58.120
and then start to focus
in on what's the plan.

00:18:58.120 --> 00:19:00.070
If you fund me, this is
what I'll be able to do,

00:19:00.070 --> 00:19:02.373
this is how I'll go about doing it.

00:19:03.470 --> 00:19:04.610
Obviously you'll want to talk about

00:19:04.610 --> 00:19:07.657
what you gain from doing
this, and it's not just,

00:19:07.657 --> 00:19:10.300
"Well, I'll be able to
complete my dissertation,"

00:19:10.300 --> 00:19:12.430
but if your dissertation is

00:19:12.430 --> 00:19:15.220
a four or five or six-year project

00:19:15.220 --> 00:19:17.050
and you're asking for
funding to go to a country

00:19:17.050 --> 00:19:19.750
for three months to gather data,

00:19:19.750 --> 00:19:22.510
what specifically will you
gather while you're there?

00:19:22.510 --> 00:19:24.810
How is this going to
inform your dissertation?

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:30.923
What piece of the overall whole
is this going to speak to?

00:19:32.650 --> 00:19:34.470
Hopefully you can get it
to that level of detail,

00:19:34.470 --> 00:19:35.303
and then of course,

00:19:35.303 --> 00:19:37.060
what is the funding agency
going to gain from this?

00:19:37.060 --> 00:19:40.020
How does my work advance your mission?

00:19:40.020 --> 00:19:41.673
What do you get out of this?

00:19:43.220 --> 00:19:46.250
And when you apply for a fellowship,

00:19:46.250 --> 00:19:49.460
make sure you know if they're
expecting any deliverables.

00:19:49.460 --> 00:19:51.740
In some cases, they're not.
They just expect you to go.

00:19:51.740 --> 00:19:54.670
You do your work and
complete your dissertation,

00:19:54.670 --> 00:19:58.030
but in other cases, they
expect a paper to be published

00:19:58.030 --> 00:19:59.910
or they expect some sort of a report

00:19:59.910 --> 00:20:02.600
or some sort of presentation.

00:20:02.600 --> 00:20:03.570
Sometimes it's open-ended,

00:20:03.570 --> 00:20:05.620
but they expect you to produce something.

00:20:07.000 --> 00:20:09.720
Find out if they're
expecting a deliverable

00:20:09.720 --> 00:20:13.023
and be sure to be up-front
about what it is you plan to do.

00:20:15.350 --> 00:20:16.750
The Fulbright-Hays application

00:20:16.750 --> 00:20:19.120
actually provides a
scoring sheet and it says

00:20:19.120 --> 00:20:22.140
you get so many points for this
and so many points for that,

00:20:22.140 --> 00:20:24.313
and one of the things that
they will score you on,

00:20:24.313 --> 00:20:26.810
it's like 10 points for your plans

00:20:26.810 --> 00:20:29.780
to share the results in your dissertation,

00:20:29.780 --> 00:20:31.570
and I've had so many students

00:20:31.570 --> 00:20:34.370
who have just missed the
cutoff for getting the grant

00:20:34.370 --> 00:20:37.480
because they never really said
anywhere in the application

00:20:37.480 --> 00:20:38.990
how they were gonna share the results

00:20:38.990 --> 00:20:41.117
from their dissertation,
and they've said to me,

00:20:41.117 --> 00:20:42.840
"Well, I'm gonna write my dissertation

00:20:42.840 --> 00:20:45.060
and then it's gonna go into the library

00:20:45.060 --> 00:20:46.300
and it will be publicly available."

00:20:46.300 --> 00:20:49.900
It's like, that's fine,
but you never said that,

00:20:49.900 --> 00:20:52.270
and so the reviewers docked you 10 points

00:20:52.270 --> 00:20:54.780
because you didn't say how you
were gonna share your results

00:20:54.780 --> 00:20:58.760
and you missed the cutoff
and there's no appeals.

00:20:58.760 --> 00:21:00.450
Make sure you understand
what they're looking for

00:21:00.450 --> 00:21:03.770
and make sure you address
those things in the proposal,

00:21:03.770 --> 00:21:05.033
and again, I can't say it enough,

00:21:05.033 --> 00:21:06.500
just think about the audience.

00:21:06.500 --> 00:21:09.450
Who's reading this? What level
of knowledge do they have?

00:21:09.450 --> 00:21:12.470
Think about the level of detail
that you need to provide,

00:21:12.470 --> 00:21:14.540
the rationale for why you're doing this,

00:21:14.540 --> 00:21:16.420
and the methods that you're going to use

00:21:16.420 --> 00:21:17.563
to get the work done.

00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:23.920
If they have a separate
personal statement,

00:21:23.920 --> 00:21:24.830
hopefully, first of all,

00:21:24.830 --> 00:21:26.870
the agency has provided information about

00:21:26.870 --> 00:21:29.920
what they would like to
see in a personal statement

00:21:29.920 --> 00:21:31.660
so that it will be tailored to the needs

00:21:31.660 --> 00:21:32.880
of the funding agency,

00:21:32.880 --> 00:21:34.380
but that's not always the case.

00:21:34.380 --> 00:21:35.857
Sometimes they will say,

00:21:35.857 --> 00:21:38.690
"And please provide a
one-page personal statement,"

00:21:38.690 --> 00:21:41.970
and you're left to guess
what they're looking for.

00:21:41.970 --> 00:21:44.710
Usually it's good to think about this as

00:21:46.930 --> 00:21:50.250
it's a description of the
experiences you've had,

00:21:50.250 --> 00:21:52.480
the professional and
educational experiences,

00:21:52.480 --> 00:21:54.710
maybe the personal experiences,

00:21:54.710 --> 00:21:56.743
that have led you to this moment.

00:21:58.223 --> 00:22:00.318
You've explained in the research proposal

00:22:00.318 --> 00:22:02.055
what it is you want to study.

00:22:02.055 --> 00:22:05.560
How did you get here? How
did you come onto this topic?

00:22:05.560 --> 00:22:09.260
What are the things that have
motivated you to do this work

00:22:09.260 --> 00:22:10.610
and what are the experiences

00:22:10.610 --> 00:22:12.773
that have prepared you to do this work?

00:22:13.970 --> 00:22:16.610
It's often the case that if
they're going to fund you,

00:22:16.610 --> 00:22:18.920
they want to see that
this is not just something

00:22:18.920 --> 00:22:20.640
that you're doing for six months

00:22:20.640 --> 00:22:23.400
and then you're gonna go
off and do something else.

00:22:23.400 --> 00:22:25.030
They want to see a journey

00:22:25.904 --> 00:22:27.710
and that there's a
motivation to do this work

00:22:27.710 --> 00:22:29.653
and that if they fund you to do this,

00:22:30.510 --> 00:22:31.690
that this is an investment

00:22:31.690 --> 00:22:33.310
that's going to pay
dividends down the road,

00:22:33.310 --> 00:22:34.770
that you're going to continue
to do this kind of work,

00:22:34.770 --> 00:22:37.390
that you really have a passion

00:22:37.390 --> 00:22:40.050
for what it is you're trying to pursue,

00:22:40.050 --> 00:22:42.310
and that we'll fund you now

00:22:43.311 --> 00:22:44.640
and this will help advance your career

00:22:44.640 --> 00:22:46.550
and then you'll continue
to do these sorts of things

00:22:46.550 --> 00:22:48.650
and that will promote our mission as well,

00:22:49.680 --> 00:22:52.380
and one of the things
that can be very helpful

00:22:52.380 --> 00:22:53.950
in the personal statement

00:22:53.950 --> 00:22:57.390
is any solid examples that you can provide

00:22:57.390 --> 00:22:59.580
of the kinds of skills or competencies

00:22:59.580 --> 00:23:01.680
that you would need to carry out the work.

00:23:05.710 --> 00:23:08.247
There's a lot of people who like to say,

00:23:08.247 --> 00:23:10.000
"Oh, I'm a real leader,"

00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:12.088
or, "I'm very adaptable

00:23:12.088 --> 00:23:15.350
and if the situation
on the ground changes,

00:23:15.350 --> 00:23:18.020
I'll be able to roll
with the circumstances

00:23:18.020 --> 00:23:21.010
and make some changes."

00:23:21.010 --> 00:23:22.270
That's all well and good to say,

00:23:22.270 --> 00:23:24.690
but if you can provide
examples of a time in the past

00:23:24.690 --> 00:23:26.270
when you've actually had to adapt

00:23:26.270 --> 00:23:28.943
or when you've shown those
leadership qualities,

00:23:29.830 --> 00:23:34.350
that's golden, that's really
what they're looking for,

00:23:34.350 --> 00:23:38.310
and it doesn't need to be a
comprehensive list of examples.

00:23:38.310 --> 00:23:41.610
Even one solid example can go really far

00:23:41.610 --> 00:23:43.623
and can put you ahead of people who say,

00:23:44.480 --> 00:23:45.470
they say they can do this,

00:23:45.470 --> 00:23:47.743
but have they ever done it in the past?

00:23:48.890 --> 00:23:50.860
Providing examples of things
that would be important

00:23:50.860 --> 00:23:52.943
to the funding agency is good,

00:23:54.420 --> 00:23:56.930
and just as you want to
talk about your motivation

00:23:56.930 --> 00:23:59.770
to do this work and how you came to this,

00:23:59.770 --> 00:24:03.380
something about your career
aspirations or the next step

00:24:03.380 --> 00:24:05.590
or where you're going to go from here.

00:24:05.590 --> 00:24:08.490
After you fund me to do
this and I do X, Y and Z,

00:24:08.490 --> 00:24:10.003
where am I going to take this?

00:24:11.390 --> 00:24:13.330
Again, it's not true of every agency,

00:24:13.330 --> 00:24:15.140
but a lot of them are going to want to see

00:24:15.140 --> 00:24:18.410
that whatever it is
they're funding you to do

00:24:18.410 --> 00:24:20.213
is a path on a journey.

00:24:21.230 --> 00:24:22.630
It's a piece of the puzzle.

00:24:22.630 --> 00:24:23.940
They want to see where you're coming from,

00:24:23.940 --> 00:24:25.610
they want to see where you're going to,

00:24:25.610 --> 00:24:28.020
and they want to see how
this funded experience

00:24:28.020 --> 00:24:31.853
is going to fit into
that larger trajectory.

00:24:36.280 --> 00:24:38.580
You've all had references.

00:24:38.580 --> 00:24:41.060
You had to get three to
get into graduate school,

00:24:41.060 --> 00:24:43.070
so you've all done this before.

00:24:43.070 --> 00:24:45.590
I think the biggest
question I get from people

00:24:45.590 --> 00:24:47.403
is things like,

00:24:48.467 --> 00:24:50.740
"Gee, I feel like I should have my advisor

00:24:50.740 --> 00:24:51.810
write a letter for me,

00:24:51.810 --> 00:24:53.927
but I've only started
working with the advisor

00:24:53.927 --> 00:24:54.910
two or three months ago.

00:24:54.910 --> 00:24:57.130
They don't know me that well.

00:24:57.130 --> 00:24:59.260
Certainly they can talk about
the importance of my work,

00:24:59.260 --> 00:25:00.580
but they don't know me personally

00:25:00.580 --> 00:25:01.640
and they can't say a lot of things

00:25:01.640 --> 00:25:04.340
and they certainly don't
know anything about my past."

00:25:05.430 --> 00:25:06.770
Most, again, not all,

00:25:06.770 --> 00:25:09.970
but most of these applications
are going to let you submit

00:25:09.970 --> 00:25:13.290
more than one reference, usually three.

00:25:13.290 --> 00:25:14.183
That's typical.

00:25:15.273 --> 00:25:19.240
You should think about it
in terms of between them,

00:25:19.240 --> 00:25:21.300
the three referees should
be able to speak about

00:25:21.300 --> 00:25:23.130
various aspects of your character.

00:25:23.130 --> 00:25:24.930
Usually the funding agency wants to know

00:25:24.930 --> 00:25:26.890
that the work that you're proposing to do

00:25:26.890 --> 00:25:28.320
is in fact important,

00:25:28.320 --> 00:25:30.700
that it's going to advance
the field in some way

00:25:30.700 --> 00:25:33.423
or fill in some gap in
knowledge in the field.

00:25:34.300 --> 00:25:36.220
They want to know that
you have the ability

00:25:36.220 --> 00:25:37.660
to carry out the work.

00:25:37.660 --> 00:25:39.920
It's one thing to show
us something, and yes,

00:25:39.920 --> 00:25:41.920
this is very exciting and
we're really enthused by this,

00:25:41.920 --> 00:25:43.150
but are you the person to do it?

00:25:43.150 --> 00:25:45.963
Are you actually
qualified to do this work?

00:25:47.350 --> 00:25:49.960
Reinforcing what I said
about the personal statement,

00:25:49.960 --> 00:25:52.080
what is your motivation to do this?

00:25:52.080 --> 00:25:53.990
And often information about

00:25:53.990 --> 00:25:57.050
what kind of support is available to you,

00:25:57.050 --> 00:25:59.570
not financial support so
much as intellectual support.

00:25:59.570 --> 00:26:03.463
Is there somebody to assist you
in doing this, to guide you?

00:26:04.790 --> 00:26:06.720
And maybe something about your personality

00:26:06.720 --> 00:26:07.953
or your character.

00:26:08.953 --> 00:26:09.930
Are you highly motivated?

00:26:09.930 --> 00:26:12.340
Do you have a stick-to-itiveness?

00:26:12.340 --> 00:26:14.690
If you say you're gonna do
something, do you get it done?

00:26:14.690 --> 00:26:16.960
Do you have the flexibility of mind

00:26:16.960 --> 00:26:19.880
to adapt to situations,

00:26:19.880 --> 00:26:21.390
and if you run into problems,

00:26:21.390 --> 00:26:23.530
are you going to be able
to overcome those problems

00:26:23.530 --> 00:26:26.010
and see this project through?

00:26:26.010 --> 00:26:27.900
It's often the case
that if you think about

00:26:27.900 --> 00:26:30.650
three people who might write for you,

00:26:30.650 --> 00:26:33.180
one of them really knows about
the work that you're doing

00:26:33.180 --> 00:26:35.110
and why this is important,

00:26:35.110 --> 00:26:37.350
but they don't know
you as well personally,

00:26:37.350 --> 00:26:39.730
and maybe somebody else has
done field work with you

00:26:39.730 --> 00:26:43.830
and maybe you reported to
them in a difficult situation

00:26:43.830 --> 00:26:45.270
and they can talk about your character

00:26:45.270 --> 00:26:47.080
and your ability to overcome obstacles,

00:26:47.080 --> 00:26:48.498
but they're not in your field.

00:26:48.498 --> 00:26:50.060
They don't have anything to do

00:26:50.060 --> 00:26:52.540
with the kind of work you're doing.

00:26:52.540 --> 00:26:57.540
Collectively they can give a
360 view of you as an applicant

00:26:57.770 --> 00:26:59.300
and that's really what
you want to think about

00:26:59.300 --> 00:27:02.150
if you're trying to decide
who should be writing for you.

00:27:05.890 --> 00:27:08.590
I said earlier that the
second most important thing

00:27:08.590 --> 00:27:11.990
for you to do was to understand
the goals of the agency.

00:27:11.990 --> 00:27:13.660
It's also important for your referees

00:27:13.660 --> 00:27:15.430
to understand the goals of the agency

00:27:15.430 --> 00:27:17.070
and to make sure that their letters

00:27:17.070 --> 00:27:19.163
are tailored to those goals.

00:27:21.770 --> 00:27:24.300
I had a student come to me some years ago.

00:27:24.300 --> 00:27:26.767
He was applying to the NSF and he said,

00:27:26.767 --> 00:27:28.890
"One of my recommenders said,

00:27:28.890 --> 00:27:30.590
they read my proposal and they said,

00:27:30.590 --> 00:27:32.490
'Oh, take out all the stuff about

00:27:32.490 --> 00:27:33.557
the outreach that you've been doing

00:27:33.557 --> 00:27:35.160
and your teaching experience.

00:27:35.160 --> 00:27:36.390
The NSF doesn't care about that.

00:27:36.390 --> 00:27:38.670
They just want to see the science.'"

00:27:38.670 --> 00:27:40.290
It's true that the science is important

00:27:40.290 --> 00:27:41.930
and they're not going to fund a project

00:27:41.930 --> 00:27:44.070
if they're not excited about the science,

00:27:44.070 --> 00:27:45.550
but if all you have is good science,

00:27:45.550 --> 00:27:47.540
that's good enough for
an honorable mention.

00:27:47.540 --> 00:27:49.650
It's not gonna get you the NSF fellowship

00:27:50.520 --> 00:27:53.550
because there is this
huge outreach component.

00:27:53.550 --> 00:27:55.470
There's the scientific literacy component

00:27:55.470 --> 00:27:56.930
to the NSF's mission.

00:27:56.930 --> 00:27:58.430
You have to have good science

00:27:58.430 --> 00:28:00.840
and you also have to show
that you're willing to go out

00:28:00.840 --> 00:28:03.460
and share your science
with non-scientists.

00:28:03.460 --> 00:28:06.260
They're both equally important
parts of the application.

00:28:07.620 --> 00:28:12.400
If the funding agency has an
FAQ that you can print out

00:28:12.400 --> 00:28:14.310
or if you can type up a cheat sheet

00:28:15.322 --> 00:28:16.907
for the referees and say,

00:28:16.907 --> 00:28:18.410
"These are the things that are important

00:28:18.410 --> 00:28:19.490
to this funding agency.

00:28:19.490 --> 00:28:21.360
This is what they're looking for,"

00:28:21.360 --> 00:28:25.780
and ask them to tailor
their letter to those needs,

00:28:25.780 --> 00:28:27.230
it just reinforces the idea

00:28:27.230 --> 00:28:30.270
that this agency has a reason
for providing the funding.

00:28:30.270 --> 00:28:32.220
We're trying to speak to those reasons.

00:28:35.100 --> 00:28:37.470
In some cases you may be required

00:28:37.470 --> 00:28:40.040
to submit a budget with your application.

00:28:40.040 --> 00:28:41.790
I don't have a whole
lot to say about that.

00:28:41.790 --> 00:28:43.360
Everybody's going to be
a little bit different.

00:28:43.360 --> 00:28:47.370
Just a few tips I would
say is, first of all,

00:28:47.370 --> 00:28:48.770
before you write up your budget,

00:28:48.770 --> 00:28:51.393
be aware of what the
agency is willing to fund.

00:28:53.100 --> 00:28:55.210
They may say, "We'll fund stipend

00:28:55.210 --> 00:28:57.330
and travel expenses and health insurance,

00:28:57.330 --> 00:28:58.540
but we don't fund tuition

00:28:58.540 --> 00:29:00.797
and we don't fund research expenses."

00:29:02.440 --> 00:29:05.430
That said, they may want
a comprehensive budget.

00:29:05.430 --> 00:29:07.140
They may want to know the total cost

00:29:07.140 --> 00:29:09.500
of what it is you're trying to do,

00:29:09.500 --> 00:29:12.320
even though they're only
willing to fund a portion of it,

00:29:12.320 --> 00:29:14.110
or they only want you to submit a budget

00:29:14.110 --> 00:29:16.180
for the parts that
they're willing to fund.

00:29:16.180 --> 00:29:17.700
Just know what their rules are

00:29:17.700 --> 00:29:20.230
and make sure that
whatever it is you provide

00:29:21.350 --> 00:29:23.020
speaks to that,

00:29:23.020 --> 00:29:25.030
and don't be afraid to ask for help

00:29:25.030 --> 00:29:26.250
from people in your department.

00:29:26.250 --> 00:29:27.620
Others have done this before,

00:29:27.620 --> 00:29:30.000
so don't feel like you're
entirely on your own

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:31.600
trying to put together a budget.

00:29:32.590 --> 00:29:33.670
When you do the budgeting,

00:29:33.670 --> 00:29:36.230
it's this sort of push-pull between

00:29:36.230 --> 00:29:38.860
how much should I ask for,
is this gonna be enough,

00:29:38.860 --> 00:29:40.350
I don't want to ask too much.

00:29:40.350 --> 00:29:42.220
You certainly don't
want to pad the budget.

00:29:42.220 --> 00:29:44.920
If the agency is looking
at three applications,

00:29:44.920 --> 00:29:47.743
they say, "Well, this
person's asking for 10,000

00:29:47.743 --> 00:29:49.329
and this person's asking for 5,000,

00:29:49.329 --> 00:29:50.760
this person's asking for 4,000.

00:29:50.760 --> 00:29:53.770
We like all three projects equally.

00:29:53.770 --> 00:29:57.220
We can fund this one or
we can fund these two."

00:29:57.220 --> 00:30:00.590
They might go for the
two, but at the same time,

00:30:00.590 --> 00:30:03.320
you also want to make sure
you ask for what you need.

00:30:03.320 --> 00:30:05.960
There's no point in submitting a proposal

00:30:05.960 --> 00:30:08.430
if you know it's gonna cost $500 a month

00:30:08.430 --> 00:30:10.840
to rent an apartment in
the place that you're going

00:30:10.840 --> 00:30:12.830
and you only ask for $200 a month.

00:30:12.830 --> 00:30:14.010
Unless you have another plan

00:30:14.010 --> 00:30:16.430
for coming up with the other 300,

00:30:16.430 --> 00:30:18.283
you're not gonna have a place to live.

00:30:19.275 --> 00:30:21.620
Make sure you ask for what you need,

00:30:21.620 --> 00:30:23.630
try not to ask for more
than what you need,

00:30:23.630 --> 00:30:27.030
and use the people around
you who have done this before

00:30:27.030 --> 00:30:29.660
to get some feedback about what they will

00:30:29.660 --> 00:30:31.277
and about what they've done

00:30:31.277 --> 00:30:33.027
and what they think is appropriate.

00:30:34.050 --> 00:30:38.760
It's also useful to know whether
the agency will allow you

00:30:38.760 --> 00:30:41.820
to seek additional funding
if they support you.

00:30:41.820 --> 00:30:44.260
In some cases, Fulbright's
a good example of this,

00:30:44.260 --> 00:30:47.436
they'll say that, "We're
giving you a stipend

00:30:47.436 --> 00:30:49.330
and we're giving you a travel budget,

00:30:49.330 --> 00:30:51.730
so we expect you not
to be getting a stipend

00:30:51.730 --> 00:30:54.170
or travel support from other sources.

00:30:54.170 --> 00:30:56.730
On the other hand, we're not
paying any research expenses,

00:30:56.730 --> 00:30:58.100
so if you can find somebody else

00:30:58.100 --> 00:31:00.230
who's willing to fund
the research expenses,

00:31:00.230 --> 00:31:01.547
that's perfectly fine."

00:31:02.750 --> 00:31:04.630
There are other agencies that

00:31:04.630 --> 00:31:05.920
they're giving you a small fellowship.

00:31:05.920 --> 00:31:09.240
It's $5,000 in stipend.
They know this isn't enough.

00:31:09.240 --> 00:31:13.000
They expect you to go
out and get other awards

00:31:13.000 --> 00:31:14.520
and it might even be helpful in that case

00:31:14.520 --> 00:31:15.637
to say in the application,

00:31:15.637 --> 00:31:19.430
"These are the other places
that I'm reaching out to

00:31:19.430 --> 00:31:20.957
to try to get funding,"

00:31:23.320 --> 00:31:28.320
but if you said that your total
budget is gonna be $50,000,

00:31:28.550 --> 00:31:30.060
that's what you need to get this done

00:31:30.060 --> 00:31:32.910
and you're applying to
this agency for $10,000,

00:31:32.910 --> 00:31:35.930
they may say, "Well, this is
fine, we're excited by this,

00:31:35.930 --> 00:31:38.380
but we want to know where he's
getting the other 40,000 from

00:31:38.380 --> 00:31:39.920
before we commit our money

00:31:39.920 --> 00:31:42.830
'cause 10,000 isn't
going to allow him or her

00:31:42.830 --> 00:31:45.580
to do what he or she needs to do."

00:31:45.580 --> 00:31:48.100
Again, you have to read the guidelines

00:31:48.100 --> 00:31:50.270
and see what they say

00:31:50.270 --> 00:31:52.530
and then try to tailor the application

00:31:52.530 --> 00:31:54.343
to what they've asked for.

00:31:57.496 --> 00:31:58.380
Just a couple of tips.

00:31:58.380 --> 00:32:01.780
It's useful to keep transcripts handy,

00:32:01.780 --> 00:32:03.080
to keep your CV up to date.

00:32:03.080 --> 00:32:05.350
I find that when you're
applying for these things,

00:32:05.350 --> 00:32:07.410
it's always just a lot easier to do

00:32:07.410 --> 00:32:10.300
if the supporting
materials are ready to go,

00:32:10.300 --> 00:32:12.410
if all you really need to
do is write the proposal

00:32:12.410 --> 00:32:14.630
and then slip everything else in,

00:32:14.630 --> 00:32:17.650
and again, read the rules.

00:32:17.650 --> 00:32:19.236
Don't just rely on what
other people have told you

00:32:19.236 --> 00:32:21.880
or what you think you
know about the agency.

00:32:21.880 --> 00:32:25.020
There's gonna be some sort
of a call for applications.

00:32:25.020 --> 00:32:28.050
There'll be a solicitation document.

00:32:28.050 --> 00:32:30.890
Read the instructions.
Read them carefully.

00:32:30.890 --> 00:32:32.320
Do they want a CV?

00:32:32.320 --> 00:32:34.100
Do they want you to enter
all of this information

00:32:34.100 --> 00:32:36.300
into an online form field?

00:32:36.300 --> 00:32:37.440
They've asked for transcripts.

00:32:37.440 --> 00:32:39.530
Do they have to be official transcripts?

00:32:39.530 --> 00:32:41.410
Will unofficial transcripts do?

00:32:41.410 --> 00:32:44.460
What happens if you can only
obtain electronic transcripts?

00:32:44.460 --> 00:32:45.660
How do they handle that?

00:32:46.690 --> 00:32:47.890
If this is one of those grants

00:32:47.890 --> 00:32:50.523
where they do have some
short answer essays,

00:32:51.550 --> 00:32:53.130
they're asking specific questions,

00:32:53.130 --> 00:32:55.620
don't feel like you need to
repeat what's in the essays

00:32:55.620 --> 00:32:57.160
in the statements.

00:32:57.160 --> 00:32:59.830
You can use your space wisely

00:32:59.830 --> 00:33:01.680
and if you've got something in one place,

00:33:01.680 --> 00:33:04.053
use the other space to
talk about other things.

00:33:05.200 --> 00:33:08.480
Certainly be aware of
application deadlines.

00:33:08.480 --> 00:33:10.260
Most of them are pretty solid.

00:33:10.260 --> 00:33:12.560
Most places will shut
down their applications

00:33:12.560 --> 00:33:15.610
at the deadline so you
can't submit things late,

00:33:15.610 --> 00:33:17.050
and remember that you don't have

00:33:17.050 --> 00:33:18.540
full control over everything.

00:33:18.540 --> 00:33:19.990
If you've requested transcripts

00:33:19.990 --> 00:33:23.960
or you've requested
letters of recommendation,

00:33:23.960 --> 00:33:25.620
you can tell people when
these things are due,

00:33:25.620 --> 00:33:27.940
but they're gonna work
on their own timetables.

00:33:27.940 --> 00:33:31.690
You want to make sure you let
people know well in advance.

00:33:31.690 --> 00:33:34.250
Make sure they know
what the deadlines are.

00:33:34.250 --> 00:33:36.730
Don't just tell them, "This
is due on October 30th."

00:33:36.730 --> 00:33:39.130
Make sure they know this is
due at noon on October 30th,

00:33:39.130 --> 00:33:41.190
at five o'clock on October 30th,

00:33:41.190 --> 00:33:43.260
so they're not thinking
it's a midnight deadline

00:33:43.260 --> 00:33:44.890
and they try to submit it and find out

00:33:44.890 --> 00:33:46.040
that they can't get in,

00:33:47.630 --> 00:33:49.220
and silly stuff.

00:33:49.220 --> 00:33:50.920
I've seen so many students get caught up

00:33:50.920 --> 00:33:52.670
with they've submitted something,

00:33:52.670 --> 00:33:54.090
but they've used the wrong font

00:33:54.090 --> 00:33:57.460
or they violated the guidelines

00:33:57.460 --> 00:34:00.060
on margins or something.

00:34:00.060 --> 00:34:01.610
Again, just read the instructions.

00:34:01.610 --> 00:34:03.270
Make sure you know what
they're looking for

00:34:03.270 --> 00:34:04.310
and make sure you conform

00:34:04.310 --> 00:34:06.560
to whatever their
application guidelines are.

00:34:09.650 --> 00:34:10.640
Just a few final thoughts

00:34:10.640 --> 00:34:12.720
and then we'll open this up for questions.

00:34:12.720 --> 00:34:14.210
It's not always the case,

00:34:14.210 --> 00:34:16.880
but it's often the case
that if you've submitted

00:34:16.880 --> 00:34:19.600
the proposal,

00:34:19.600 --> 00:34:21.750
once they give you an answer,
they may give you feedback,

00:34:21.750 --> 00:34:22.970
that you may get comments.

00:34:22.970 --> 00:34:26.110
You may get a review of your application.

00:34:26.110 --> 00:34:28.353
If you do, make use of it.

00:34:29.350 --> 00:34:31.050
Read what they have to say.

00:34:31.050 --> 00:34:32.870
If you didn't get funded,

00:34:32.870 --> 00:34:35.130
feel free to rewrite

00:34:35.130 --> 00:34:37.680
and respond to the critiques
that they've provided

00:34:37.680 --> 00:34:39.250
and then resubmit.

00:34:39.250 --> 00:34:41.120
I've seen, even at the faculty level,

00:34:41.120 --> 00:34:44.957
I saw a grant a few months
ago, the agency said,

00:34:44.957 --> 00:34:47.500
"We think that this proposal is too large.

00:34:47.500 --> 00:34:50.340
We think it would benefit
from being more focused

00:34:50.340 --> 00:34:51.850
on a smaller number of faculty,"

00:34:51.850 --> 00:34:55.130
in a sort of a sub-field
within this larger field,

00:34:55.130 --> 00:34:56.560
and they resubmitted the grant

00:34:56.560 --> 00:34:59.040
and they basically had
a long explanation about

00:34:59.040 --> 00:35:00.510
how the funding agency was wrong

00:35:00.510 --> 00:35:03.890
and why it needed to be a larger grant.

00:35:03.890 --> 00:35:06.050
Certainly they're within
their rights to do that,

00:35:06.050 --> 00:35:09.720
but I don't think that
was a very wise approach.

00:35:09.720 --> 00:35:11.017
If the funding agency had said,

00:35:11.017 --> 00:35:12.620
"We liked what you submitted.

00:35:12.620 --> 00:35:15.520
We're willing to fund this
but not willing to fund that,"

00:35:15.520 --> 00:35:18.510
don't come back and try
to get that funded again.

00:35:18.510 --> 00:35:20.260
Respond to what they've had to say.

00:35:22.600 --> 00:35:24.560
Don't ignore the small awards.

00:35:24.560 --> 00:35:25.892
There's a lot of grants out there,

00:35:25.892 --> 00:35:29.320
the 25, 30, $35,000
fellowships and so forth

00:35:29.320 --> 00:35:32.764
or travel awards and those
are great if you can get them,

00:35:32.764 --> 00:35:34.800
but the small grants help too.

00:35:34.800 --> 00:35:37.910
Somebody who can cobble
together enough small awards

00:35:37.910 --> 00:35:39.830
has the equivalent of a large award,

00:35:39.830 --> 00:35:41.620
and again, like I said at the beginning,

00:35:41.620 --> 00:35:43.260
funding begets funding.

00:35:43.260 --> 00:35:44.740
If somebody sees that you're out there

00:35:44.740 --> 00:35:46.223
and you're applying for this
and that and the other thing

00:35:46.223 --> 00:35:47.920
and you've been successful here

00:35:47.920 --> 00:35:50.310
and you've been successful there,

00:35:50.310 --> 00:35:51.190
it greases the wheels.

00:35:51.190 --> 00:35:53.830
You're more likely to get
that big award down the road

00:35:53.830 --> 00:35:56.010
if you have a couple of
small awards to your name.

00:35:56.010 --> 00:35:58.223
We had a student here
a few years ago from,

00:35:59.961 --> 00:36:02.830
I forget exactly, it was
a Scandinavian country,

00:36:02.830 --> 00:36:05.570
and I think he fully funded
in two years of his education.

00:36:05.570 --> 00:36:07.120
I don't think any of the awards he had

00:36:07.120 --> 00:36:09.350
were worth more than $8,000,

00:36:09.350 --> 00:36:11.010
but he had four or five of them,

00:36:11.010 --> 00:36:13.567
and together, they pulled a full stipend.

00:36:13.567 --> 00:36:17.020
It was really pretty
impressive, (laughing)

00:36:17.020 --> 00:36:19.470
and again, just going back to
what I said at the beginning,

00:36:19.470 --> 00:36:20.520
you should be proactive

00:36:20.520 --> 00:36:22.890
and you should be applying for these now,

00:36:22.890 --> 00:36:24.870
not after you get out of Yale

00:36:24.870 --> 00:36:26.897
and suddenly you need the external support

00:36:26.897 --> 00:36:29.880
and you don't have the backstop.

00:36:29.880 --> 00:36:33.050
You do not want the first
application that you send in

00:36:33.050 --> 00:36:35.620
to be the one that if it's not funded,

00:36:35.620 --> 00:36:37.720
you can't do the work that you want to do.

00:36:40.270 --> 00:36:42.380
Please, if you're looking at the support

00:36:42.380 --> 00:36:44.167
that Yale's giving you and you're saying,

00:36:44.167 --> 00:36:46.480
"Oh, there's all this money
from the MacMillan Center

00:36:46.480 --> 00:36:49.130
and I got this stipend support.

00:36:49.130 --> 00:36:50.660
I don't really need to be applying,"

00:36:50.660 --> 00:36:53.903
this is just a great
time to get the practice.

00:36:55.010 --> 00:36:56.810
You may be successful. You may not be.

00:36:56.810 --> 00:36:57.970
If you're not,

00:36:57.970 --> 00:36:59.670
you can still go out and
do what you need to do

00:36:59.670 --> 00:37:02.340
because we do have other forms of support,

00:37:02.340 --> 00:37:04.170
but the first grant is always the hardest.

00:37:04.170 --> 00:37:05.960
They get easier to write.

00:37:05.960 --> 00:37:09.253
The more you get, the
easier they get to get more.

00:37:10.490 --> 00:37:14.450
Take advantage now of the sort
of safety net that you have

00:37:14.450 --> 00:37:16.863
and please, please go
out and look for these.

00:37:18.460 --> 00:37:19.293
I'll stop here.

00:37:19.293 --> 00:37:22.330
If there are any questions,
I'll be happy to answer them,

00:37:22.330 --> 00:37:24.430
and I have contact information here.

00:37:24.430 --> 00:37:27.683
Please feel free to contact me later

00:37:27.683 --> 00:37:29.270
if you're applying for something

00:37:29.270 --> 00:37:31.370
and you have more specific questions

00:37:31.370 --> 00:37:33.763
than might be appropriate in this setting.

00:37:37.990 --> 00:37:40.120
- Thank you so much, Dean Harper-Mangels.

00:37:40.120 --> 00:37:42.770
This has been great and just
what we were hoping for.

00:37:44.070 --> 00:37:46.430
If any attendees do have questions

00:37:46.430 --> 00:37:48.050
that you've not yet
submitted in the Q and A,

00:37:48.050 --> 00:37:49.790
please feel free to type
those into the Q and A

00:37:49.790 --> 00:37:53.910
and we'll do our best to get
them asked and answered here.

00:37:53.910 --> 00:37:55.610
We do have some questions already.

00:37:56.470 --> 00:37:59.050
They range from the particular
to the more general,

00:37:59.050 --> 00:38:00.510
but let me just start with this one

00:38:00.510 --> 00:38:03.033
since it's chronologically
first in some sense.

00:38:04.490 --> 00:38:07.890
One person asks, "Is your
first year in graduate school

00:38:07.890 --> 00:38:10.810
too early to start applying
for grants and fellowships?"

00:38:10.810 --> 00:38:12.210
What's your opinion on that?

00:38:15.310 --> 00:38:16.930
- Let me, I'm gonna stop the share,

00:38:16.930 --> 00:38:18.260
but I'll put it back up at the end

00:38:18.260 --> 00:38:21.110
if anybody still needs
my contact information.

00:38:21.110 --> 00:38:24.530
I think it's never too
early to start applying.

00:38:24.530 --> 00:38:26.480
Of course, it depends on,

00:38:26.480 --> 00:38:29.380
everybody comes into graduate
school in a different place.

00:38:30.300 --> 00:38:32.460
Some people already know

00:38:32.460 --> 00:38:34.220
what they want to do
for their dissertation

00:38:34.220 --> 00:38:37.010
and they know exactly
why they've come here

00:38:37.010 --> 00:38:40.080
and who they want to work
with, and that's great,

00:38:40.080 --> 00:38:43.450
and other people have general ideas,

00:38:43.450 --> 00:38:46.080
but they're gonna spend the
first two years kicking around

00:38:46.080 --> 00:38:48.710
and taking classes and going to seminars

00:38:48.710 --> 00:38:50.660
and trying to figure out

00:38:52.110 --> 00:38:54.113
where their passion lies.

00:38:55.810 --> 00:38:57.090
What I would say is,

00:38:57.090 --> 00:39:01.170
and just as we have a lot of students

00:39:01.170 --> 00:39:02.130
who are in different places,

00:39:02.130 --> 00:39:04.010
the fellowships are all
in different places.

00:39:04.010 --> 00:39:07.060
There are some fellowships
that are very specific,

00:39:07.060 --> 00:39:09.220
they support very specific types of work,

00:39:09.220 --> 00:39:11.840
and there are others that
are very much more general,

00:39:11.840 --> 00:39:15.070
that are really to support students

00:39:15.070 --> 00:39:18.470
who one day will be
experts in their field,

00:39:18.470 --> 00:39:19.850
but they're not necessarily designed

00:39:19.850 --> 00:39:24.733
to support a specific course of study.

00:39:25.569 --> 00:39:27.590
The NSF Graduate Research Fellowship

00:39:27.590 --> 00:39:28.920
is a great example of this

00:39:28.920 --> 00:39:31.120
and I think a lot of
people don't understand

00:39:32.452 --> 00:39:34.260
that they don't just fund
the natural sciences,

00:39:34.260 --> 00:39:36.500
they fund the social sciences as well,

00:39:36.500 --> 00:39:41.500
and you write a proposal for the NSF

00:39:42.770 --> 00:39:44.630
and they're evaluating that proposal.

00:39:44.630 --> 00:39:46.640
They want you to propose something

00:39:46.640 --> 00:39:48.650
that is interesting and innovative,

00:39:48.650 --> 00:39:50.290
that you have a plan for doing,

00:39:50.290 --> 00:39:52.970
but largely what they're doing
is evaluating your ability

00:39:52.970 --> 00:39:54.900
to come up with a project.

00:39:54.900 --> 00:39:59.900
They're evaluating your
ability to design a study

00:39:59.930 --> 00:40:02.880
and go out and conduct that work.

00:40:02.880 --> 00:40:07.300
They don't necessarily expect
you to do this specific study.

00:40:07.300 --> 00:40:09.360
In fact, a lot of the people
who are applying for this

00:40:09.360 --> 00:40:10.870
are actually college seniors

00:40:10.870 --> 00:40:13.080
who at the time they're
submitting the application

00:40:13.080 --> 00:40:14.920
don't actually know
where they're going to be

00:40:14.920 --> 00:40:16.163
in graduate school yet.

00:40:18.870 --> 00:40:19.703
Something like that,

00:40:19.703 --> 00:40:21.730
and I should say there
are a few others like that

00:40:21.730 --> 00:40:24.580
where you really have to apply
in your first or second year.

00:40:24.580 --> 00:40:26.640
They're intended to be early-year awards.

00:40:26.640 --> 00:40:30.020
I've encouraged students
to look for those,

00:40:30.020 --> 00:40:31.839
but obviously as you move on,

00:40:31.839 --> 00:40:33.170
as you move into your
third and fourth year,

00:40:33.170 --> 00:40:34.520
you've written a prospectus,

00:40:34.520 --> 00:40:36.910
you have a defined dissertation,

00:40:36.910 --> 00:40:37.850
then you're going to be applying

00:40:37.850 --> 00:40:39.450
for different types of grants

00:40:39.450 --> 00:40:43.643
that are much more specific
and much more utilitarian.

00:40:44.620 --> 00:40:46.470
We're supporting work in this field

00:40:46.470 --> 00:40:47.530
where we give you travel money

00:40:47.530 --> 00:40:49.160
so you can go to this country

00:40:49.160 --> 00:40:51.260
and gather the data you
need for your dissertation.

00:40:51.260 --> 00:40:53.140
Just understand that there's a wide range

00:40:53.140 --> 00:40:55.140
of grants out there,

00:40:55.140 --> 00:40:57.350
and so while the types of
things that you will look at

00:40:57.350 --> 00:41:00.440
will change as you transition
to graduate school,

00:41:00.440 --> 00:41:02.290
it's never too soon to start looking.

00:41:03.210 --> 00:41:05.730
- I think you just
anticipated or maybe answered

00:41:05.730 --> 00:41:08.030
this other question that
we received which is,

00:41:09.480 --> 00:41:11.980
have you ever seen people
apply to grants and fellowships

00:41:11.980 --> 00:41:14.640
that end up changing
their career trajectories,

00:41:14.640 --> 00:41:16.210
not just allowing them to do

00:41:16.210 --> 00:41:17.660
what they thought they were going to do

00:41:17.660 --> 00:41:21.550
at the moment that they applied
to that grant or fellowship?

00:41:21.550 --> 00:41:24.550
- Yeah, I think, especially
with a lot of the travel grants.

00:41:24.550 --> 00:41:27.863
I see this all the time
with Fulbright fellows.

00:41:28.990 --> 00:41:33.420
They propose to do X and
they get funded to do X

00:41:33.420 --> 00:41:35.480
and they go to whatever
country they're going to do,

00:41:35.480 --> 00:41:36.780
and while they're there,

00:41:36.780 --> 00:41:39.440
they just have this amazing experience

00:41:39.440 --> 00:41:41.710
and they're either turned
on to other resources

00:41:41.710 --> 00:41:42.700
that they weren't aware of

00:41:42.700 --> 00:41:44.950
or they run into problems
that they weren't expecting

00:41:44.950 --> 00:41:46.410
and they need to shift gears,

00:41:46.410 --> 00:41:47.993
and by the time they come back,

00:41:49.333 --> 00:41:51.870
their research has gone
in a completely different

00:41:51.870 --> 00:41:53.573
but totally exciting direction,

00:41:54.670 --> 00:41:56.550
and so, yeah,

00:41:56.550 --> 00:42:00.240
they can really be
transformative in that sense.

00:42:00.240 --> 00:42:01.640
Obviously doesn't happen to everybody.

00:42:01.640 --> 00:42:02.770
Some people just go and do

00:42:02.770 --> 00:42:04.160
what they said they were going to do,

00:42:04.160 --> 00:42:06.700
and that's fine too, but yeah,

00:42:06.700 --> 00:42:10.090
it's not at all uncommon
for people to find their,

00:42:10.090 --> 00:42:12.720
the process of doing the research

00:42:12.720 --> 00:42:14.040
takes them in a very different direction

00:42:14.040 --> 00:42:17.120
than they thought they were
going in when they started.

00:42:17.120 --> 00:42:17.953
- That's great.

00:42:17.953 --> 00:42:19.680
I have some mechanical questions for ya

00:42:19.680 --> 00:42:21.410
that have popped up here as well.

00:42:21.410 --> 00:42:23.460
You mentioned a cheat
sheet for recommenders

00:42:23.460 --> 00:42:26.370
at one point in your talk,
which was really very helpful.

00:42:26.370 --> 00:42:29.950
One of the questions here
is about how an applicant,

00:42:29.950 --> 00:42:34.940
a student can help their
advisor to best support them

00:42:34.940 --> 00:42:36.350
in the application process.

00:42:36.350 --> 00:42:38.390
For example, by writing the strongest

00:42:38.390 --> 00:42:41.963
letter of recommendation that
the recommender can write.

00:42:43.080 --> 00:42:44.270
The question that, for example,

00:42:44.270 --> 00:42:46.540
should I be providing a set of documents

00:42:46.540 --> 00:42:49.130
to my recommenders in
advance that describe things

00:42:49.130 --> 00:42:52.863
like my personality, my
project, my motivation?

00:42:55.060 --> 00:42:56.250
Beyond the FAQ,

00:42:57.270 --> 00:42:59.050
how should I describe the fellowship

00:42:59.050 --> 00:43:01.400
or grant opportunity to my recommenders?

00:43:01.400 --> 00:43:03.990
Any further thoughts
about what cheat sheets

00:43:03.990 --> 00:43:07.290
or document packets for
recommenders might look like?

00:43:07.290 --> 00:43:08.430
- Yeah, that's great.

00:43:08.430 --> 00:43:12.813
I would say that there are two things

00:43:12.813 --> 00:43:16.050
that would be very helpful
to your recommenders.

00:43:16.050 --> 00:43:18.630
The first is just understanding
the goals of the agency.

00:43:18.630 --> 00:43:20.143
What are they looking for?

00:43:21.060 --> 00:43:24.770
And it may be that there's
just a page you can print out

00:43:24.770 --> 00:43:26.860
from the agency itself that
says these are our goals

00:43:26.860 --> 00:43:30.230
or there's an FAQ or something, or if not,

00:43:30.230 --> 00:43:31.630
if you've read their materials

00:43:31.630 --> 00:43:33.270
and you don't have something easy

00:43:33.270 --> 00:43:35.520
that you can point them to,

00:43:35.520 --> 00:43:36.817
then you can type something up and say,

00:43:36.817 --> 00:43:38.440
"This is what this agency is supporting.

00:43:38.440 --> 00:43:40.000
These are the sorts of
things that they said

00:43:40.000 --> 00:43:42.210
they were looking for in an application.

00:43:42.210 --> 00:43:45.497
These are the points I've tried
to hit on in my proposal,"

00:43:46.670 --> 00:43:48.680
and then the second part of that is

00:43:48.680 --> 00:43:51.520
just your personal
qualities, and I would say,

00:43:51.520 --> 00:43:53.690
I don't know that I would give an advisor

00:43:53.690 --> 00:43:56.870
a cheat sheet that says
this is my personality

00:43:56.870 --> 00:44:00.810
or something along those
lines, but certainly,

00:44:00.810 --> 00:44:02.740
I talked about some of
those tangible examples

00:44:02.740 --> 00:44:04.940
that you might put into
a personal statement

00:44:06.080 --> 00:44:07.117
and if you want to tell them,

00:44:07.117 --> 00:44:09.620
"These are some of the
things that I have done

00:44:09.620 --> 00:44:11.650
that I think makes me
qualified to do this.

00:44:11.650 --> 00:44:12.807
These are some of the things

00:44:12.807 --> 00:44:15.330
and these experiences that I've had

00:44:15.330 --> 00:44:18.150
that I think will be of
interest to this agency

00:44:18.150 --> 00:44:21.967
and that I intend to
promote in my essays,"

00:44:22.940 --> 00:44:25.563
to the degree that you
feel comfortable doing so,

00:44:26.690 --> 00:44:30.263
please try to speak to
these in your letter.

00:44:32.060 --> 00:44:32.893
- That's great.

00:44:32.893 --> 00:44:34.170
You gave some wonderful advice about

00:44:34.170 --> 00:44:37.440
getting feedback on the personal statement

00:44:37.440 --> 00:44:40.380
or other parts of the
application that'll be submitted,

00:44:40.380 --> 00:44:43.157
and one person asks, they say,

00:44:43.157 --> 00:44:45.790
"I worry that I'm getting
the wrong kind of feedback

00:44:45.790 --> 00:44:47.113
on my application.

00:44:48.437 --> 00:44:51.050
When I speak to my peers in my department

00:44:51.050 --> 00:44:52.910
or my academic advisors,

00:44:52.910 --> 00:44:55.100
I feel like they continue
to push me to be narrow

00:44:55.100 --> 00:44:56.780
and more specific.

00:44:56.780 --> 00:45:00.110
How should I balance that sort of feedback

00:45:00.110 --> 00:45:03.450
from experts and peers,
some of whom, of course,

00:45:03.450 --> 00:45:06.730
who never received a
grant like this before,

00:45:06.730 --> 00:45:10.370
with the kind of feedback
I can receive from,

00:45:10.370 --> 00:45:12.707
for example, the Graduate Writing Lab?"

00:45:15.320 --> 00:45:16.930
- Yeah, that's tricky.

00:45:16.930 --> 00:45:21.290
Part of the process
you're in now as a student

00:45:21.290 --> 00:45:26.290
and becoming a full-fledged
academic is sort of,

00:45:27.330 --> 00:45:29.440
it's that transition from being a student

00:45:29.440 --> 00:45:32.130
to being the master.

00:45:32.130 --> 00:45:35.850
It's sort of knowing when
you have to take the advice

00:45:35.850 --> 00:45:37.990
that's being given to you
by people who are older

00:45:37.990 --> 00:45:40.710
and more experienced and more credentialed

00:45:40.710 --> 00:45:43.137
and when it's time to stand up and say,

00:45:43.137 --> 00:45:46.010
"No, I actually know more
about this than you do,

00:45:46.010 --> 00:45:47.910
and I appreciate your advice,

00:45:47.910 --> 00:45:49.517
but we're going my way with it."

00:45:50.480 --> 00:45:52.260
This is part of that process,

00:45:52.260 --> 00:45:57.260
and I place great deference on
what the faculty have to say

00:45:57.710 --> 00:45:59.250
and the advice that they give,

00:45:59.250 --> 00:46:01.510
but I've also seen that they don't know

00:46:01.510 --> 00:46:03.850
everything about everything,

00:46:03.850 --> 00:46:06.790
and I gave that one
example of the professor

00:46:06.790 --> 00:46:08.727
who was writing for the
NSF applicant who said,

00:46:08.727 --> 00:46:10.890
"Oh yeah, take out all the stuff about

00:46:10.890 --> 00:46:12.510
your activities outside of the lab.

00:46:12.510 --> 00:46:13.530
They don't care about that."

00:46:13.530 --> 00:46:16.000
No, they really do care about that

00:46:16.000 --> 00:46:18.450
and taking that out would've
been a huge mistake.

00:46:20.881 --> 00:46:24.187
What you need to weigh
is the degree to which,

00:46:25.273 --> 00:46:28.920
is the advice you're getting

00:46:28.920 --> 00:46:31.220
simply a matter of personal preference

00:46:31.220 --> 00:46:32.870
or is it really going against

00:46:32.870 --> 00:46:34.963
what you know the agency is looking for?

00:46:36.232 --> 00:46:38.300
And if it's really going against

00:46:38.300 --> 00:46:39.880
what the agency is looking for,

00:46:39.880 --> 00:46:42.480
then you have to go against that advice.

00:46:42.480 --> 00:46:44.477
You have to go your own way and say,

00:46:44.477 --> 00:46:46.727
"No, this is what the
agency is looking for."

00:46:49.610 --> 00:46:51.943
The people who work in the field,

00:46:53.660 --> 00:46:55.560
they know all the nuances about the field

00:46:55.560 --> 00:46:58.360
and if they were reviewing
an application in the field,

00:46:58.360 --> 00:47:00.890
they would expect you to be knowledgeable

00:47:00.890 --> 00:47:02.393
about those nuances,

00:47:03.520 --> 00:47:05.480
but if you know that this is being read

00:47:05.480 --> 00:47:08.180
by people who are not
experts in the field,

00:47:08.180 --> 00:47:09.530
then you need to step up a level

00:47:09.530 --> 00:47:11.520
and you really need to give
them the bigger picture

00:47:11.520 --> 00:47:14.610
and understand that the nuances
that are so important to you

00:47:14.610 --> 00:47:17.870
and to your advisor and to
other people in your circle

00:47:17.870 --> 00:47:19.840
are going to be lost on people

00:47:19.840 --> 00:47:22.550
who are actually making
these funding decisions,

00:47:22.550 --> 00:47:23.383
but it's hard.

00:47:23.383 --> 00:47:24.790
There's no easy answer to that.

00:47:26.850 --> 00:47:28.390
You're going to get conflicting advice

00:47:28.390 --> 00:47:32.660
and part of becoming a professional is

00:47:32.660 --> 00:47:34.597
evaluating that advice and saying,

00:47:34.597 --> 00:47:36.510
"Yeah, in this circumstance,
this is good advice.

00:47:36.510 --> 00:47:39.010
This is bad advice. This
is what I'm going to do."

00:47:40.440 --> 00:47:41.470
- I think I have another question

00:47:41.470 --> 00:47:43.672
for which there's not
necessarily an easy answer.

00:47:43.672 --> 00:47:44.804
(Robert and Steve laughing)

00:47:44.804 --> 00:47:48.310
It's a question about tone
and about selling oneself

00:47:48.310 --> 00:47:50.023
or trumpeting oneself.

00:47:51.260 --> 00:47:53.640
Question is, "I'm worried about coming off

00:47:53.640 --> 00:47:57.290
as overly confident in my applications,

00:47:57.290 --> 00:47:59.980
which is funny because I don't
feel altogether confident

00:47:59.980 --> 00:48:01.440
about my work.

00:48:01.440 --> 00:48:04.010
How do I think about striking a balance

00:48:04.010 --> 00:48:07.437
between modesty on the
one hand," parentheses,

00:48:07.437 --> 00:48:10.420
"I am still learning,
and mastery on the other,

00:48:10.420 --> 00:48:11.930
as I am an emerging professional

00:48:11.930 --> 00:48:13.430
and you should give me money."

00:48:14.860 --> 00:48:16.820
- That's a great question.

00:48:16.820 --> 00:48:20.440
I would say, obviously, I
don't want to oversell this,

00:48:20.440 --> 00:48:22.713
but this is not the time to be humble.

00:48:24.460 --> 00:48:28.430
You are a developing
professional in this field.

00:48:28.430 --> 00:48:31.290
You are becoming a knowledge
expert on your subject

00:48:32.840 --> 00:48:35.323
and you want to promote yourself as such.

00:48:36.290 --> 00:48:39.010
There are certain grants and
I have a lot of experience

00:48:39.010 --> 00:48:40.870
working with Fulbright fellows,

00:48:40.870 --> 00:48:43.310
so I keep coming back to that.

00:48:43.310 --> 00:48:46.580
One of the things that
Fulbright wants to know

00:48:46.580 --> 00:48:48.800
is that people are adaptable.

00:48:48.800 --> 00:48:52.110
They understand that if
you're going to Tanzania

00:48:52.110 --> 00:48:55.930
to do some research and
there's civil strife there

00:48:55.930 --> 00:48:57.540
and there's a disease outbreak,

00:48:57.540 --> 00:48:59.790
you might not be able to do

00:48:59.790 --> 00:49:01.660
what you've proposed to
do once you get there

00:49:01.660 --> 00:49:04.330
and situation's unpredictable.

00:49:04.330 --> 00:49:08.070
They understand that things
can change very suddenly.

00:49:08.070 --> 00:49:09.210
They want to know that the people

00:49:09.210 --> 00:49:10.650
that they're sending overseas

00:49:10.650 --> 00:49:13.070
are going to be able to
deal with that situation

00:49:13.070 --> 00:49:15.360
and you're not going
to just suddenly become

00:49:15.360 --> 00:49:18.863
paralyzed and ineffective.

00:49:21.844 --> 00:49:24.017
For a proposal like that,
it's perfectly fine to say,

00:49:24.017 --> 00:49:26.360
"This is what I wanna do.
These are the resources I need.

00:49:26.360 --> 00:49:28.380
This is what I expect to
be able to accomplish.

00:49:28.380 --> 00:49:30.290
I'm aware of these situations

00:49:30.290 --> 00:49:32.270
and aware that there may be circumstances

00:49:32.270 --> 00:49:34.520
that could prevent this from happening.

00:49:34.520 --> 00:49:35.360
Should that happen,

00:49:35.360 --> 00:49:38.147
here's some of the directions
that I could possibly go in."

00:49:39.460 --> 00:49:42.910
That's one level at which you
might just acknowledge that,

00:49:42.910 --> 00:49:44.450
yeah, I've got these great plans,

00:49:44.450 --> 00:49:47.490
but sometimes when the
rubber hits the road,

00:49:47.490 --> 00:49:50.320
plans fall through and
I've got ideas about

00:49:50.320 --> 00:49:52.520
how I would adapt to
that and run with that.

00:49:53.413 --> 00:49:55.937
What you don't want to
do is certainly say,

00:49:55.937 --> 00:49:57.750
"Well, here's what I want to do.

00:49:57.750 --> 00:50:00.810
I'm not sure if I really have
the expertise to do this,"

00:50:00.810 --> 00:50:04.130
or, "I'm not sure if I'm good
enough to get this done."

00:50:04.130 --> 00:50:05.530
If you're not sure, then they're not sure

00:50:05.530 --> 00:50:06.900
and they're not going to fund you.

00:50:06.900 --> 00:50:08.730
That's the end of it.

00:50:08.730 --> 00:50:11.560
To the degree that you're
proposing to do things

00:50:11.560 --> 00:50:13.800
where you really don't have expertise,

00:50:13.800 --> 00:50:15.720
so I'll give you a good example of this.

00:50:15.720 --> 00:50:18.370
I had a student some years back,

00:50:18.370 --> 00:50:20.730
again, applying for a Fulbright.

00:50:20.730 --> 00:50:22.580
She was an engineer.

00:50:22.580 --> 00:50:24.030
She wanted to go to Indonesia

00:50:24.030 --> 00:50:27.420
and she was interested in water filtration

00:50:27.420 --> 00:50:30.830
and delivery of clean
water to rural communities.

00:50:30.830 --> 00:50:33.020
Part of her project was
actually working in a lab

00:50:33.020 --> 00:50:35.940
at the university there studying
water filtration systems

00:50:35.940 --> 00:50:38.220
and thinking about ways
to make them cheaper,

00:50:38.220 --> 00:50:40.980
more affordable so they
could be mass-produced

00:50:40.980 --> 00:50:43.770
and got them out to the
public, and as an engineer,

00:50:43.770 --> 00:50:46.033
she was well-qualified to do that work.

00:50:46.920 --> 00:50:48.970
There was a second
component to her proposal

00:50:48.970 --> 00:50:52.200
in which she was going to do
a social science component.

00:50:52.200 --> 00:50:54.110
She was going to go out
into the communities

00:50:54.110 --> 00:50:57.170
and find out who was using
the water filtration systems

00:50:57.170 --> 00:50:59.670
that were already available to them.

00:50:59.670 --> 00:51:01.610
Who was using them, who wasn't using them,

00:51:01.610 --> 00:51:03.510
what were the barriers to using them,

00:51:03.510 --> 00:51:04.570
how effective were they,

00:51:04.570 --> 00:51:07.020
how often were people
getting sick, and so forth.

00:51:08.260 --> 00:51:10.040
She did not have a background

00:51:10.040 --> 00:51:13.220
in conducting social science
research, and in fact,

00:51:13.220 --> 00:51:15.500
she didn't have the local language

00:51:15.500 --> 00:51:18.420
that might allow her to go out
into these rural communities.

00:51:18.420 --> 00:51:20.530
She was fine working at the university,

00:51:20.530 --> 00:51:22.950
but not necessarily in these communities

00:51:22.950 --> 00:51:24.063
outside of the city,

00:51:25.070 --> 00:51:27.593
but she had a great plan
because she was paired up

00:51:27.593 --> 00:51:29.750
with an NGO that was
working in the country

00:51:29.750 --> 00:51:31.190
that was interested in this project

00:51:31.190 --> 00:51:32.890
and she was going to partner with them

00:51:32.890 --> 00:51:35.610
to do the social science
component of the project.

00:51:35.610 --> 00:51:38.097
She was able in her application to say,

00:51:38.097 --> 00:51:39.590
"There's two parts to my project.

00:51:39.590 --> 00:51:42.100
These are the skills I
have to carry out this one,

00:51:42.100 --> 00:51:43.590
and that's solid.

00:51:43.590 --> 00:51:45.670
These are the skills I
have to carry out the other

00:51:45.670 --> 00:51:46.700
and they're a little more tenuous,

00:51:46.700 --> 00:51:51.070
but here's the support that
I have to help me do that,"

00:51:51.070 --> 00:51:52.920
and she was funded for this.

00:51:52.920 --> 00:51:57.920
I think that if there's a real,
you don't want to be humble.

00:51:58.260 --> 00:52:01.210
You don't want to be saying,
"Oh, I could probably do this.

00:52:01.210 --> 00:52:02.600
Other people might be better at it."

00:52:02.600 --> 00:52:03.920
Not the time to be humble,

00:52:03.920 --> 00:52:06.480
but if there's a real
component to the application

00:52:06.480 --> 00:52:10.890
where you think, "I don't
have the skills to do this yet

00:52:10.890 --> 00:52:12.587
and anybody reading the
application is gonna see

00:52:12.587 --> 00:52:14.987
that I don't have the
skills to do this yet,"

00:52:16.100 --> 00:52:19.750
then you want to talk about
what kind of support do I have.

00:52:19.750 --> 00:52:21.690
Maybe this grant doesn't start for a year

00:52:21.690 --> 00:52:23.160
and so how am I going to get

00:52:23.160 --> 00:52:24.510
the skills I need in the next year,

00:52:24.510 --> 00:52:26.670
or what kind of support
am I getting from Yale,

00:52:26.670 --> 00:52:28.050
or what kind of support am I getting

00:52:28.050 --> 00:52:31.230
from the area where I'll
be conducting this research

00:52:31.230 --> 00:52:33.970
that will allow me to
both develop my skills

00:52:33.970 --> 00:52:35.030
and carry this out

00:52:35.030 --> 00:52:37.333
despite the lack of
experience that I have?

00:52:38.500 --> 00:52:39.410
- As you're talking, Robert,

00:52:39.410 --> 00:52:41.820
it strikes me that you're, in a sense,

00:52:41.820 --> 00:52:45.920
urging people to express confidence about

00:52:45.920 --> 00:52:47.710
the problem that they're
looking to address

00:52:47.710 --> 00:52:49.360
as being an important problem,

00:52:49.360 --> 00:52:52.340
the questions that they're
bringing to that problem,

00:52:52.340 --> 00:52:55.260
and their own ability to
address those questions.

00:52:55.260 --> 00:52:56.940
There's also a piece of
this that seems to me

00:52:56.940 --> 00:52:59.880
in which applicants are
trying to emphasize,

00:52:59.880 --> 00:53:01.460
as you say, their flexibility,

00:53:01.460 --> 00:53:04.680
or other ways to think about
this might be curiosity,

00:53:04.680 --> 00:53:06.070
willingness to change course

00:53:06.070 --> 00:53:08.670
if the answers come through
that they're not expecting,

00:53:08.670 --> 00:53:10.170
and I do sometimes feel like

00:53:10.170 --> 00:53:13.410
sometimes applicants make
too strong of an argument

00:53:13.410 --> 00:53:15.160
that they know exactly
what the answers are

00:53:15.160 --> 00:53:17.040
and they just need to go
out and find the evidence

00:53:17.040 --> 00:53:20.280
to substantiate exactly what
they know to already be true.

00:53:20.280 --> 00:53:22.560
Have you ever seen that
as a problem in anything,

00:53:22.560 --> 00:53:24.280
any students you've worked with?

00:53:24.280 --> 00:53:26.243
- Yeah, that comes up periodically.

00:53:28.738 --> 00:53:30.660
They've already decided
what the conclusion is

00:53:30.660 --> 00:53:33.230
before they've actually gathered the data

00:53:33.230 --> 00:53:35.100
and they don't leave any wiggle room

00:53:35.100 --> 00:53:37.620
for if I go out and find these sources

00:53:37.620 --> 00:53:38.980
and it's not actually matching up

00:53:38.980 --> 00:53:41.400
with what I think I'm going to find,

00:53:41.400 --> 00:53:44.850
they don't allow for that
possibility in their brain space

00:53:44.850 --> 00:53:48.250
and I think that can be a
real problem, and again,

00:53:48.250 --> 00:53:50.970
it's hard to talk about this
in the abstract because, again,

00:53:50.970 --> 00:53:54.070
every funding agency is
a little bit different.

00:53:54.070 --> 00:53:57.310
Some of these grants
are funding the student.

00:53:57.310 --> 00:54:00.590
They're funding your
development as a scholar,

00:54:00.590 --> 00:54:03.090
and so that curiosity, that flexibility,

00:54:03.090 --> 00:54:04.590
that idea that, "You know what?

00:54:04.590 --> 00:54:05.980
This project might not work out,

00:54:05.980 --> 00:54:08.510
but this person's going to
learn a lot in that process

00:54:08.510 --> 00:54:10.780
and is going to be a better
scholar for it in the future."

00:54:10.780 --> 00:54:12.760
That's great for some agencies,

00:54:12.760 --> 00:54:15.767
and others, they want a deliverable.

00:54:15.767 --> 00:54:18.500
"We're funding you to go out and get X

00:54:18.500 --> 00:54:20.440
and this is why we're
giving you the money,

00:54:20.440 --> 00:54:21.273
and if you can't get X,

00:54:21.273 --> 00:54:23.573
then we're gonna give the
money to somebody else,"

00:54:25.406 --> 00:54:28.100
and that's again one of
those things where you might,

00:54:28.100 --> 00:54:29.670
one award might be more appropriate

00:54:29.670 --> 00:54:31.730
for a second or third-year
student who's still developing

00:54:31.730 --> 00:54:33.140
but isn't sure about everything,

00:54:33.140 --> 00:54:34.330
whereas the other one might be good

00:54:34.330 --> 00:54:36.150
for a fifth or sixth-year student

00:54:36.150 --> 00:54:38.290
who's pretty far along in their research,

00:54:38.290 --> 00:54:39.930
already has drawn their conclusions,

00:54:39.930 --> 00:54:43.190
just needs to go out and
get some supporting data

00:54:43.190 --> 00:54:45.370
or fill in some gaps somewhere

00:54:45.370 --> 00:54:50.370
and it's much more
specific, detailed grant

00:54:50.620 --> 00:54:52.720
than they might otherwise be applying for.

00:54:53.590 --> 00:54:54.423
- That makes sense.

00:54:54.423 --> 00:54:55.256
I have two more questions for you

00:54:55.256 --> 00:54:56.770
and they're both pretty practical.

00:54:56.770 --> 00:55:00.807
The first person asks, basically,

00:55:00.807 --> 00:55:03.570
"How do I know what a
reasonable budget looks like

00:55:03.570 --> 00:55:06.060
that isn't, quote, padded?

00:55:06.060 --> 00:55:09.410
I'm not asking to spend the
night in a five-star hotel,

00:55:09.410 --> 00:55:12.725
but do I have to say that I
will sleep in a one-star hotel?"

00:55:12.725 --> 00:55:15.810
(Robert laughing)

00:55:15.810 --> 00:55:17.900
- The best way to answer this, I'd say,

00:55:17.900 --> 00:55:21.180
is that other people have probably gone

00:55:21.180 --> 00:55:22.350
to wherever it is you're going

00:55:22.350 --> 00:55:23.720
and had to provide a budget too,

00:55:23.720 --> 00:55:26.390
and so it's a good idea to
just talk to other people

00:55:26.390 --> 00:55:29.140
who have traveled and
see what kinds of budgets

00:55:29.140 --> 00:55:30.560
they have proposed.

00:55:30.560 --> 00:55:32.523
There's some resources online.

00:55:34.120 --> 00:55:35.680
Some of the funding agencies, for example,

00:55:35.680 --> 00:55:38.420
will have guidelines as
to what they will provide

00:55:38.420 --> 00:55:40.220
for this or that or the other thing,

00:55:42.782 --> 00:55:46.320
and if you say, "I'm going
to go to this country,

00:55:46.320 --> 00:55:48.390
and yes, the program will provide

00:55:48.390 --> 00:55:53.200
up to $500 a month for accommodations,

00:55:53.200 --> 00:55:54.990
but I actually know
somebody in the country

00:55:54.990 --> 00:55:58.240
and I won't need that.

00:55:58.240 --> 00:56:02.400
I can pay them $100 for expenses and,"

00:56:02.400 --> 00:56:04.040
the agency will appreciate the fact

00:56:04.040 --> 00:56:06.193
that you're finding ways to save money,

00:56:07.610 --> 00:56:10.230
but I would say, in terms
of knowing what to do,

00:56:10.230 --> 00:56:13.180
just talk to other
people who have applied.

00:56:13.180 --> 00:56:15.700
I think the MacMillan Center
is probably a great resource

00:56:15.700 --> 00:56:19.137
for travel abroad, where they can say,

00:56:19.137 --> 00:56:22.607
"This is what the living
expenses are in the country."

00:56:23.721 --> 00:56:24.820
Your travel expenses,

00:56:24.820 --> 00:56:27.630
go online and look at
a variety of airlines

00:56:27.630 --> 00:56:30.140
and figure out what roundtrip
tickets are gonna cost

00:56:30.140 --> 00:56:32.770
and try to pick the cheapest one

00:56:32.770 --> 00:56:36.010
that doesn't involve you
flying in a cargo plane

00:56:36.010 --> 00:56:37.483
with crates full of chickens.

00:56:38.540 --> 00:56:40.670
You don't have to cut it to the bone

00:56:40.670 --> 00:56:43.650
and have the cheapest budget
you can possibly find,

00:56:43.650 --> 00:56:47.453
but they do expect you to
cut costs where you can,

00:56:49.190 --> 00:56:51.010
but usually there are resources online

00:56:51.010 --> 00:56:52.807
and certainly resources
at the university can say,

00:56:52.807 --> 00:56:56.350
"Yes, living expenses in
this country for a traveler

00:56:56.350 --> 00:57:00.810
would be between 300 and
700 a month," for example,

00:57:00.810 --> 00:57:05.490
and so probably best then not
to come down on the $700 side.

00:57:05.490 --> 00:57:06.896
You don't have to say 300.

00:57:06.896 --> 00:57:08.170
You want to give yourself
a little room maybe,

00:57:08.170 --> 00:57:11.150
but try to come in under
500 if that's the range,

00:57:11.150 --> 00:57:12.730
but other people have done this before you

00:57:12.730 --> 00:57:15.600
and have had experience
and had budgets approved

00:57:15.600 --> 00:57:17.290
or had budgets cut,

00:57:17.290 --> 00:57:18.860
and so there should be resources out there

00:57:18.860 --> 00:57:21.183
to help you figure out what's appropriate.

00:57:22.480 --> 00:57:24.140
- Great, thank you, and the last question,

00:57:24.140 --> 00:57:25.250
the answer may be the same,

00:57:25.250 --> 00:57:26.490
which is that there are other people

00:57:26.490 --> 00:57:27.400
who can help you with this,

00:57:27.400 --> 00:57:29.883
but maybe you have particular
thoughts about who.

00:57:31.030 --> 00:57:33.717
The question is really about
CVs, and the person says,

00:57:33.717 --> 00:57:36.678
"I'm not sure if my CV
is properly formatted

00:57:36.678 --> 00:57:37.670
(Robert laughing)
or if it has

00:57:37.670 --> 00:57:40.000
the right information on it. (laughing)

00:57:40.000 --> 00:57:41.540
Do you have any suggestions about

00:57:41.540 --> 00:57:44.580
how one should get feedback on their CV

00:57:44.580 --> 00:57:46.830
to make sure that it's
professionally ready?"

00:57:48.670 --> 00:57:49.850
- First of all, I will just say

00:57:49.850 --> 00:57:53.433
that there's a lot of
different CVs out there.

00:57:54.350 --> 00:57:55.460
There's different styles.

00:57:55.460 --> 00:57:57.670
There's not necessarily
one right way to do it,

00:57:57.670 --> 00:58:01.770
and I myself, when I was
younger and applying for jobs,

00:58:01.770 --> 00:58:03.700
depending on the nature of
the job I was applying for,

00:58:03.700 --> 00:58:07.263
would move things around on
my CV, which would either,

00:58:08.640 --> 00:58:10.200
which would emphasize my research

00:58:10.200 --> 00:58:13.330
or would emphasize my teaching
experience or what have you,

00:58:13.330 --> 00:58:14.960
depending on the job I was applying for.

00:58:14.960 --> 00:58:17.390
It's not a one size fits all,

00:58:17.390 --> 00:58:20.970
but I think that the Office
for Career Strategies

00:58:20.970 --> 00:58:22.953
is a great resource for,

00:58:24.332 --> 00:58:25.720
if you contact them and
say you need some help,

00:58:25.720 --> 00:58:28.030
you need somebody to look at your CV

00:58:28.030 --> 00:58:29.730
and give you some feedback on that,

00:58:29.730 --> 00:58:32.280
they have people there that
can help you with that.

00:58:33.710 --> 00:58:36.030
- Great. Thank you so
much, Dean Harper-Mangels.

00:58:36.030 --> 00:58:39.760
That's not the most extravagant
way to end this session.

00:58:39.760 --> 00:58:42.100
I think that was rather kind of mundane,

00:58:42.100 --> 00:58:43.450
but I think a really important advice

00:58:43.450 --> 00:58:45.380
because I think it's
one of the many things

00:58:45.380 --> 00:58:47.720
that so many applicants worry about

00:58:47.720 --> 00:58:48.787
when they're making these applications,

00:58:48.787 --> 00:58:51.260
and they're, as you well know,

00:58:51.260 --> 00:58:52.820
many people are doing these applications

00:58:52.820 --> 00:58:54.490
while they're very busy with other work,

00:58:54.490 --> 00:58:58.630
and so trying to figure
out ways to explain

00:58:58.630 --> 00:59:02.300
what people ought to be
doing and how is just so key,

00:59:02.300 --> 00:59:04.010
and so thank you so much

00:59:04.010 --> 00:59:06.340
for making such a compelling argument

00:59:06.340 --> 00:59:09.710
for the importance of
making these applications.

00:59:09.710 --> 00:59:11.330
I think you're absolutely right

00:59:11.330 --> 00:59:15.390
that applying in these ways really is key

00:59:15.390 --> 00:59:18.140
for people to learn how
to promote their own work

00:59:18.140 --> 00:59:21.050
and that funding begets
funding, as you say,

00:59:21.050 --> 00:59:23.620
and that for those reasons and others,

00:59:23.620 --> 00:59:27.601
encouraging people in the greater
RITM network and elsewhere

00:59:27.601 --> 00:59:29.510
to be looking for these opportunities,

00:59:29.510 --> 00:59:31.760
I think, is incredibly beneficial,

00:59:31.760 --> 00:59:34.240
even as it can be hard and difficult

00:59:34.240 --> 00:59:37.910
in the course of one's regular,
very busy life as well.

00:59:37.910 --> 00:59:39.570
- And I'll say,

00:59:39.570 --> 00:59:42.040
it's the mundane aspects
of the application

00:59:42.040 --> 00:59:44.060
that often people find daunting.

00:59:44.060 --> 00:59:45.220
They know what they're doing,

00:59:45.220 --> 00:59:46.810
they understand their research,

00:59:46.810 --> 00:59:48.350
but then they find out
they have to pull in

00:59:48.350 --> 00:59:50.950
all of these other pieces of information

00:59:50.950 --> 00:59:52.620
and that gets a lot of people caught up.

00:59:52.620 --> 00:59:54.843
I remember the first time,

00:59:56.230 --> 00:59:58.700
when I worked at Northwestern,
before I came to Yale,

00:59:58.700 --> 00:59:59.997
and my director came to me and he said,

00:59:59.997 --> 01:00:02.900
"We're going to apply for an
institutional training grant,"

01:00:02.900 --> 01:00:05.260
and these are large
multimillion-dollar awards

01:00:05.260 --> 01:00:08.180
that fund multiple
faculty, multiple students.

01:00:08.180 --> 01:00:11.070
The proposal itself, the
scientific component to it,

01:00:11.070 --> 01:00:13.300
it was only 10 to 15 pages.

01:00:13.300 --> 01:00:15.960
The entire proposal is about 200 pages

01:00:15.960 --> 01:00:18.650
'cause suddenly you
need data about students

01:00:18.650 --> 01:00:21.340
going back 10 years and
how many people came in

01:00:21.340 --> 01:00:22.840
and where did they wind up going

01:00:22.840 --> 01:00:25.750
and what's your training
program look like,

01:00:25.750 --> 01:00:29.370
and that was really hard
to do the first time,

01:00:29.370 --> 01:00:31.760
but I gotta tell you, by the
time we did the fifth one,

01:00:31.760 --> 01:00:34.610
we had a process, we had
a lot of the data already,

01:00:34.610 --> 01:00:37.720
and so once we had the intellectual
component put together,

01:00:37.720 --> 01:00:40.210
then we could just slot in
all those mundane elements

01:00:40.210 --> 01:00:42.190
and it got a lot easier to do.

01:00:42.190 --> 01:00:43.560
This is one of the things where I say

01:00:43.560 --> 01:00:45.880
it gets easier to write these
things as you go forward

01:00:45.880 --> 01:00:49.110
because as you get used to
doing the mundane parts of it,

01:00:49.110 --> 01:00:52.540
you can focus on the interesting
intellectual component

01:00:52.540 --> 01:00:54.560
and then just add in all
of that mundane stuff

01:00:54.560 --> 01:00:56.410
that needs to be part of the application

01:00:56.410 --> 01:00:59.110
and it just gets so much easier over time.

01:00:59.110 --> 01:01:00.040
- I really appreciate that,

01:01:00.040 --> 01:01:02.170
and I think this is the kind of training

01:01:02.170 --> 01:01:04.080
that we get too little of,

01:01:04.080 --> 01:01:05.410
at least on our side of the university,

01:01:05.410 --> 01:01:07.470
so thank you so much for your time today

01:01:07.470 --> 01:01:09.590
and for giving us the
benefit of your skills.

01:01:09.590 --> 01:01:11.150
I'm sure people will follow up with you,

01:01:11.150 --> 01:01:13.030
and I know you've already shared

01:01:13.030 --> 01:01:15.290
your email address with people

01:01:16.240 --> 01:01:17.800
and so I would just add my voice

01:01:17.800 --> 01:01:19.390
to encourage people to
write to you directly

01:01:19.390 --> 01:01:21.300
if there's anything that they think

01:01:21.300 --> 01:01:23.250
you might be able to help them with

01:01:23.250 --> 01:01:24.960
or direct them elsewhere.

01:01:24.960 --> 01:01:27.390
I think you've offered
yourself up as a resource,

01:01:27.390 --> 01:01:28.750
so we really appreciate that.

01:01:28.750 --> 01:01:30.470
- Yeah, please take advantage of me.

01:01:30.470 --> 01:01:32.320
This is why I'm here.

01:01:32.320 --> 01:01:34.470
If you have questions,
please get in touch.

01:01:35.390 --> 01:01:36.810
- All right. Thank you, Rob.

01:01:36.810 --> 01:01:37.643
Thank you, everybody.

01:01:37.643 --> 01:01:38.510
- [Robert] My pleasure.

01:01:38.510 --> 01:01:39.343
- Take care.

